add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Sinus Tachycardia Versus Paroxysmal Reentrant SVT',
 'During evaluation of palpitations, continuous ECG shows a regular narrow-complex rhythm gradually accelerating from 90 to 135/min as fever rises. P-wave morphology and axis match the baseline sinus tracing, and the rate falls gradually after treatment of fever and dehydration. Which mechanism best fits?',
 'Physiological sinus tachycardia',[
 ('Typical AV nodal reentrant tachycardia','AVNRT commonly starts and stops abruptly and often obscures atrial activity within or near the QRS. Gradual rate change with a physiological trigger and unchanged sinus P waves supports sinus activation instead.'),
 ('Orthodromic AV reentrant tachycardia','Orthodromic AVRT usually has abrupt paroxysms and retrograde atrial activation associated with a circuit using the AV node and an accessory pathway. The observed gradual sinus-rate trajectory is less consistent with that mechanism.'),
 ('Focal atrial tachycardia','An automatic atrial focus can warm up and cool down, so gradual change alone is not diagnostic. Here the unchanged sinus morphology and rate response to a documented physiological demand favor sinus tachycardia rather than a non-sinus focus.')],
 [S('Interpret the Whole Rate Trajectory','**Sinus tachycardia is usually a response to increased demand or altered autonomic drive.** Fever, hypovolemia, pain, hypoxemia, anemia, thyrotoxicosis and other triggers should be sought according to the presentation.',
    'A sinus-appearing P wave before each QRS, appropriate atrial axis and gradual acceleration or deceleration support the diagnosis. Comparison with the baseline tracing is useful when morphology is uncertain.',
    'Atrial and ventricular rates alone do not define the mechanism. Sinus tachycardia can be rapid, while AVNRT or AVRT can be relatively slow under medication or in older patients.'),
  S('Treat the Driver Before Suppressing the Rate','**Correct the physiological cause and reassess perfusion and symptoms.** Tachycardia may be compensatory, so reflex AV-nodal blockade can worsen output in a depleted or critically ill patient.',
    'Reentrant SVTs typically have abrupt onset and termination, whereas automatic focal AT may show warm-up and cool-down. These are clues that require integration with atrial morphology and a recorded episode.',
    'Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is considered only when a persistent symptomatic sinus-rate syndrome is not explained by a physiological or secondary cause. A febrile dehydrated patient does not meet that interpretation simply because the resting rate exceeds 100/min. POTS requires a posture-related assessment and its own criteria rather than a fever-associated tracing. Obtain a 12-lead ECG during symptoms when feasible and reconsider the mechanism if episodes remain abrupt or unexplained after the trigger resolves.')],['SVT2019'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Short-RP Regular Narrow-Complex Tachycardia',
 'A patient has abrupt recurrent regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 185/min. Retrograde atrial deflections appear just after the QRS with a very short RP interval. The baseline ECG has no delta wave. Which interpretation is most defensible before electrophysiological confirmation?',
 'Typical AVNRT is favored, but orthodromic AVRT through a concealed pathway remains possible',[
 ('Orthodromic AVRT is excluded by the normal baseline ECG','An accessory pathway that conducts only retrogradely can support orthodromic AVRT without producing a delta wave in sinus rhythm. Absence of manifest pre-excitation therefore does not exclude this circuit.'),
 ('Focal atrial tachycardia is established by a visible atrial deflection','Visible atrial activity occurs in several SVTs, including retrograde activation during AVNRT or AVRT. Its timing, morphology and response to diagnostic maneuvers matter more than visibility alone.'),
 ('Typical atrial flutter is established by the ventricular rate','Flutter with 2:1 conduction often produces a rate near 150/min, but rates vary and rate alone is not diagnostic. The supplied tracing does not demonstrate a continuing flutter cycle or additional atrial waves.')],
 [S('RP Timing Narrows the Differential','**A very short RP interval during abrupt regular SVT favors typical slow–fast AVNRT.** Near-simultaneous atrial and ventricular activation can hide retrograde P waves or produce a pseudo-r-prime in V1 or pseudo-S waves inferiorly.',
    'Orthodromic AVRT uses antegrade AV nodal/His–Purkinje conduction and retrograde accessory-pathway conduction. Its retrograde P wave may also lie shortly after the QRS, so surface timing is not an infallible circuit diagnosis.',
    'A concealed pathway conducts retrogradely without manifest antegrade pre-excitation. A normal sinus-rhythm ECG therefore cannot independently exclude accessory-pathway-mediated tachycardia.'),
  S('Use the Recording to Guide Further Assessment','**Preserve a 12-lead tracing during tachycardia and compare it with sinus rhythm.** Examine regularity, QRS width, atrial activation and onset or termination rather than relying on a rate label.',
    'Vagal maneuvers or adenosine can terminate AV-node-dependent circuits in a suitable stable patient. Termination supports nodal dependence but does not alone distinguish AVNRT from AVRT, and some focal tachycardias can also respond.',
    'EPS can establish the mechanism when recurrent symptoms justify definitive assessment or ablation. Initial management still depends on hemodynamic stability and the actual rhythm: a diagnostic hypothesis must not delay treatment of attributable shock. Explain that the likely diagnosis can be clinically useful without pretending that a short-RP strip uniquely identifies every circuit. The eventual ablation target differs between an AV nodal slow pathway and an accessory pathway, making precise mechanism assessment consequential.')],['SVT2019'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Long-RP Supraventricular Tachycardia',
 'A stable patient has regular narrow-complex tachycardia with RP longer than PR and negative P waves in the inferior leads. Adenosine terminates the episode. Which conclusion is most defensible from these findings alone?',
 'Atypical AVNRT, slowly conducting-pathway AVRT and adenosine-sensitive atrial tachycardia remain possible',[
 ('Adenosine termination establishes atypical AVNRT','Atypical AVNRT can terminate with adenosine, but slowly conducting accessory-pathway AVRT and some focal AT mechanisms can do so as well. The drug response is not a unique circuit identifier.'),
 ('Negative inferior P waves establish permanent junctional reciprocating tachycardia','PJRT often has long RP and inferiorly negative retrograde P waves, but atypical AVNRT and low atrial foci can resemble this pattern. Additional electrophysiological evidence is needed before assigning that specific pathway mechanism.'),
 ('RP longer than PR establishes focal atrial tachycardia','Focal AT is an important long-RP diagnosis, but delayed retrograde conduction during atypical AVNRT or AVRT can produce the same surface timing relationship. RP classification narrows rather than completes the differential.')],
 [S('Delayed Atrial Timing Has Several Mechanisms','**Long-RP tachycardia describes atrial activation late after the preceding QRS, with RP longer than the following PR.** It does not by itself establish whether atrial activity drives or follows the ventricular cycle.',
    'Fast–slow or slow–slow AVNRT can have delayed retrograde nodal activation. PJRT is orthodromic AVRT using a slowly conducting, decremental retrograde accessory pathway, commonly producing inferiorly negative P waves.',
    'A focal atrial source can produce a similar apparent relationship. Sinus tachycardia and other junctional mechanisms remain relevant in the appropriate clinical context; onset, offset and atrial morphology help refine the assessment.'),
  S('Adenosine Response Is Not a Mechanism Label','**Adenosine termination is compatible with more than one long-RP circuit.** AV-node-dependent reentry can stop, while certain adenosine-sensitive focal atrial mechanisms can also terminate.',
    'Record the full termination sequence and determine whether atrial activity persists when ventricular conduction is interrupted. EPS can resolve the mechanism when recurrent symptoms or an ablation decision make certainty consequential.',
    'Incessant long-RP tachycardia can cause tachycardia-mediated ventricular dysfunction, particularly with PJRT or incessant AT. Evaluate burden and ventricular function rather than assuming a modest rate makes it harmless. Treatment and ablation targets differ between a slow nodal pathway, an accessory pathway and an atrial focus. Avoid applying a definitive procedural label from one P-wave polarity or an adenosine response; preserve the working differential until sufficient evidence is available.')],['SVT2019'],
 table=(['Mechanism','Reason for long RP','Important qualification'],[
 ['Atypical AVNRT','Delayed retrograde nodal activation','Inferiorly negative P waves can resemble PJRT'],
 ['PJRT','Slow decremental retrograde accessory pathway','No baseline delta wave is required'],
 ['Focal AT','Atrial focus with its own activation timing','Some forms terminate with adenosine']]))

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Adenosine Unmasks Continuing Atrial Tachycardia',
 'During a regular narrow-complex tachycardia, adenosine causes several nonconducted atrial impulses. Discrete abnormal P waves continue at the same cycle length, separated by an isoelectric baseline, and one-to-one ventricular conduction then resumes. Which interpretation best fits the observed response?',
 'An atrial tachycardia continuing independently of transient AV nodal block',[
 ('Orthodromic AVRT continuing through the blocked AV node','The AV node is the antegrade limb of ordinary orthodromic AVRT. Sustained atrial activity at an unchanged cycle despite interruption of that limb argues against this circuit as the explanation.'),
 ('Sinus tachycardia established by adenosine resistance','Sinus activity can continue during transient AV block, but adenosine resistance does not establish sinus origin. The abnormal discrete P-wave morphology supports a non-sinus atrial source in this recording.'),
 ('Typical AVNRT established by resumption of one-to-one conduction','AVNRT can rarely continue with block below its circuit, so absent ventricular responses alone do not settle every case. Here the independently continuing discrete abnormal atrial rhythm favors AT, and resumption of conduction is not proof of nodal reentry.')],
 [S('Inspect the Atria During the Ventricular Pause','**Adenosine can reveal atrial activity by transiently reducing AV conduction.** A continuing organized atrial rhythm may become visible when fewer QRS complexes obscure it.',
    'Discrete abnormal P waves with intervening isoelectric segments support focal AT in the appropriate context. Flutter can also persist during AV block, but typically shows a macroreentrant atrial pattern; surface appearance is not infallible, especially with scar-related rhythms.',
    'The atrial cycle length and activation pattern provide the diagnostic information. Merely observing a short ventricular pause or a lower displayed heart rate does not prove that the tachycardia terminated.'),
  S('Interpret AV Block With Appropriate Limits','**Ordinary orthodromic AVRT needs antegrade conduction through the AV node.** Interruption usually stops that circuit, whereas an atrial rhythm can continue and later regain ventricular conduction.',
    'AVNRT can occasionally persist despite block in an output pathway, so ventricular block by itself is not an absolute exclusion of every nodal circuit. Integrate the atrial recording, timing and other electrophysiological evidence.',
    'Some focal ATs terminate with adenosine; others merely become easier to identify. Preserve the diagnostic strip and choose subsequent treatment according to the actual rhythm, symptoms, burden and ventricular function. Do not repeatedly escalate adenosine simply because a continuing atrial rhythm fails to terminate. For recurrent or incessant symptomatic focal AT, specialist evaluation and ablation can be appropriate. Acute hemodynamic compromise still requires immediate rhythm-directed management rather than prolonged diagnostic drug challenges.')],['SVT2019'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Focal Atrial Tachycardia',
 'A patient has repeated narrow-complex tachycardia at 140–160/min. Each episode has one consistent P-wave morphology different from sinus rhythm, with an isoelectric interval between P waves. The atrial rate gradually accelerates at onset, and occasional AV block occurs without changing atrial timing. Which diagnosis is most likely?',
 'Focal atrial tachycardia',[
 ('Typical AV nodal reentrant tachycardia','Typical AVNRT usually has abrupt onset and near-simultaneous atrial and ventricular activation. Persistent independent abnormal atrial activity and warm-up favor an atrial source rather than that usual nodal circuit.'),
 ('Orthodromic AV reentrant tachycardia','Ordinary orthodromic AVRT needs AV conduction as part of its circuit. Continued atrial tachycardia through AV block, together with non-sinus P waves and gradual acceleration, supports a focal atrial mechanism.'),
 ('Multifocal atrial tachycardia','MAT has changing P-wave morphologies and irregular atrial timing. A single reproducible non-sinus morphology with an organized atrial cycle instead supports a single dominant atrial activation source.')],
 [S('Recognize an Organized Non-Sinus Atrial Rhythm','**Focal AT produces atrial activation spreading from a localized source.** Discrete P waves differing from sinus morphology, often with intervening isoelectric segments, support the diagnosis.',
    'Automatic activity can show warm-up and cool-down. Triggered activity and localized reentry are other mechanisms, so a focal ECG appearance does not prove that every such tachycardia is automatic.',
    'The ventricular response depends on AV conduction and may be slower or irregular despite an organized atrial rhythm. Continuing atrial activity during AV block helps separate the atrial driver from an ordinary AVRT circuit.'),
  S('Assess Burden and Select Treatment','**Incessant or frequently recurrent AT can cause tachycardia-mediated cardiomyopathy.** Assess symptoms, duration, ventricular function and reversible triggers instead of judging severity from one rate measurement.',
    'Acute treatment depends on stability and mechanism. Adenosine can terminate some focal ATs or expose atrial activity without termination. Beta blockade or verapamil/diltiazem may help selected stable patients, with attention to hypotension, ventricular function and contraindications.',
    'Catheter ablation is an important definitive option for recurrent symptomatic focal AT, particularly incessant AT associated with ventricular dysfunction. Mapping identifies the source; P-wave morphology offers clues but cannot replace invasive localization when ablation is performed. Atrial scarring can complicate the ECG distinction from macroreentry, and an isoelectric interval is not absolute proof against flutter. Reassess ventricular function after rhythm control when tachycardia-mediated dysfunction is suspected; improvement supports the causal contribution but does not exclude additional myocardial disease.')],['SVT2019'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Multifocal Atrial Tachycardia',
 'During an exacerbation of COPD, a patient has an irregular narrow-complex rhythm at 125/min. A single lead shows at least three distinct P-wave morphologies, variable P–P and PR intervals, and isoelectric segments between atrial deflections. Which diagnosis best fits?',
 'Multifocal atrial tachycardia',[
 ('Atrial fibrillation','AF has disorganized atrial activation without consistently identifiable discrete P waves. Irregularity alone is insufficient: the multiple discrete P morphologies and intervening isoelectric baseline support MAT.'),
 ('Atrial flutter with variable AV conduction','Variable conduction can make flutter ventricular intervals irregular, but the underlying atrial cycle is usually organized and repetitive. Changing P-wave morphologies and irregular atrial intervals favor MAT.'),
 ('Sinus tachycardia with frequent atrial premature beats','Sinus rhythm with ectopy retains a dominant sinus sequence interrupted by premature beats. A sustained irregular atrial rhythm with several recurring morphologies and no dominant sinus cycle is more consistent with MAT.')],
 [S('Use Atrial Morphology to Explain the Irregularity','**MAT is a rapid irregular atrial rhythm with at least three distinct P-wave morphologies in the same lead.** The conventional rate distinction from wandering atrial pacemaker is ==greater than 100/min==.',
    'P–P, PR and R–R intervals can vary, while recognizable atrial depolarizations remain separated by isoelectric segments. Confirm that artefact or superimposed T waves are not being counted as extra morphologies.',
    'Pulmonary disease, hypoxemia, pulmonary hypertension, electrolyte disturbances and theophylline exposure are relevant associations. The clinical setting supports the diagnosis but does not replace the ECG criteria.'),
  S('Treat the Underlying Illness First','**Correct the precipitating respiratory or systemic disorder and potassium or magnesium abnormalities.** Review potentially contributory drugs while maintaining adequate treatment of the underlying pulmonary disease.',
    'IV magnesium can be useful even when the measured level is normal. If symptomatic rapid conduction persists, selected beta blockers or verapamil/diltiazem can slow the ventricular response; choose according to bronchospasm, pressure, LV function and other contraindications.',
    'Electrical cardioversion and conventional rhythm drugs generally do not provide durable control of this multifocal atrial process. MAT alone does not establish the AF-related indication for anticoagulation; look for any separate indication rather than treating every irregular pulse as AF. Follow clinical recovery and repeat the ECG when needed. Persistent drug-refractory tachycardia with associated ventricular dysfunction warrants specialist assessment, while an acute pulmonary trigger should not lead directly to invasive treatment without an attempt to correct that driver.')],['SVT2019'],
 table=(['Rhythm','Atrial finding','Timing clue'],[
 ['MAT','At least 3 discrete P morphologies in one lead','Irregular P–P; rate >100/min'],
 ['Wandering atrial pacemaker','Multiple discrete P morphologies','Similar pattern at a slower rate'],
 ['AF','No consistent discrete P sequence','Irregular ventricular response unless another mechanism intervenes'],
 ['Flutter with variable block','Repeating organized atrial activation','Irregular ventricular conduction with organized atrial cycle']]))

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Atrial Flutter With Concealed Two-to-One Conduction',
 'A regular narrow-complex tachycardia runs at 150/min. During a monitored diagnostic maneuver, ventricular conduction briefly slows and continuous atrial activity at 300/min becomes visible, with repetitive negative deflections in the inferior leads. Which diagnosis best explains the original rhythm?',
 'Atrial flutter with two-to-one AV conduction',[
 ('Typical AV nodal reentrant tachycardia','AVNRT can obscure atrial activity near the QRS, but the newly visible atrial cycle is twice the original ventricular rate and continues independently as conduction slows. This supports an atrial driver rather than ordinary one-to-one nodal reentry.'),
 ('Orthodromic AV reentrant tachycardia','Ordinary orthodromic AVRT requires atria and ventricles in its circuit. A continuing atrial rhythm at 300/min while the ventricular response slows is inconsistent with the usual one-to-one accessory-pathway circuit.'),
 ('Focal atrial tachycardia with one-to-one conduction','Focal AT can continue through AV block, but one-to-one conduction would not explain the original atrial-to-ventricular ratio of two. Continuous repetitive flutter activity further favors macroreentry over a discrete focal P-wave pattern.')],
 [S('Find the Hidden Atrial Cycle','**Flutter with 2:1 conduction can masquerade as a regular SVT because alternate atrial deflections are concealed by QRS or T waves.** A ventricular rate near 150/min is a useful prompt to inspect the atria, not a diagnostic criterion by itself.',
    'The usual atrial rate is approximately 250–330/min, but medications, scarring and other substrates can change it. Determine the actual atrial rate rather than assuming that every flutter cycle is exactly 200 ms.',
    'Counterclockwise typical flutter often produces negative inferior flutter waves and positive activity in V1. Surface morphology suggests but does not definitively establish cavotricuspid-isthmus dependence.'),
  S('Diagnostic Slowing Does Not Necessarily Terminate Flutter','**Transient AV nodal block may reveal the atrial rhythm while the macroreentrant circuit continues.** Record the full tracing to distinguish ventricular slowing from conversion to sinus rhythm.',
    'Adenosine is not routine curative treatment for flutter. When used diagnostically in uncertainty, it requires monitoring and rescue readiness because AF induction or rebound rapid conduction can occur.',
    'After recognition, assess stability, ventricular function, episode duration and thromboembolic protection before choosing rate control, cardioversion or ablation. Flutter may be difficult to rate-control, and typical flutter often responds well to CTI ablation. A successful flutter intervention does not eliminate later AF or automatically end anticoagulation requirements. The immediate diagnostic lesson is to count atrial events during slowed conduction; rate labels alone can miss the rhythm that determines subsequent treatment.')],['SVT2019'])

R['SVTUS2015']=('ACC/AHA/HRS adult SVT guideline, 2015','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2015.08.856')
add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Nonreentrant Junctional Tachycardia',
 'After cardiac surgery, an adult has narrow-complex tachycardia that gradually accelerates to 135/min and later slows gradually. Sinus P waves at 85/min march independently through the rhythm, and the QRS morphology matches normally conducted baseline beats. Which mechanism is most likely?',
 'Enhanced automaticity producing focal junctional tachycardia',[
 ('Orthodromic AV reentry through an accessory pathway','Ordinary orthodromic AVRT needs the atria and ventricles as circuit components. Sustained tachycardia with independent slower atrial activity argues against that mechanism rather than an accessory-pathway return limb.'),
 ('Typical slow–fast AV nodal reentry','AVNRT is a reentrant mechanism, usually with abrupt onset and offset. Although rare AVNRT variants can dissociate the atria, gradual rate behavior and the postoperative context favor automatic junctional tachycardia here.'),
 ('Focal atrial tachycardia with variable AV block','Focal AT requires the rapid driver to be atrial. Here the atrial rate is slower than the ventricular rhythm and independent of it, so the atria are not supplying the tachycardic impulse sequence.')],
 [S('Recognize a Junctional Driver','**Focal junctional tachycardia arises from abnormal impulse formation near the AV node or proximal His region.** A narrow QRS rhythm with AV dissociation can occur because the junctional rate exceeds the sinus rate.',
    'Warm-up and cool-down favor an automatic mechanism over abrupt reentry. Some cases have retrograde atrial conduction, even one-to-one, which can make the surface rhythm resemble AVNRT or AVRT.',
    'AV dissociation rules out ordinary AVRT but does not absolutely exclude every AVNRT variant. A careful mechanism assessment is therefore more reliable than using a single surface sign as universal proof.'),
  S('Address the Context and Hemodynamic Consequences','**Assess postoperative stress, catecholamine exposure, ischemia, electrolyte disturbance and medication toxicity.** The setting and ventricular function influence both urgency and treatment.',
    'Treat reversible drivers and obtain specialist rhythm input. Selected beta blockers or other antiarrhythmics can be used according to the clinical substrate; evidence in adults is limited, and a pediatric postoperative regimen should not be transferred automatically.',
    'Adenosine may reveal atrial nonparticipation but does not reliably terminate automatic junctional tachycardia. Electrical cardioversion is also less likely to provide durable suppression of an automatic focus than of a reentrant circuit. Persistent symptomatic or hemodynamically important disease can require specialized treatment, including selected ablation with attention to AV-block risk. Avoid reflexly selecting slow-pathway ablation merely because the rhythm arises near the AV junction; the target and procedural risks depend on the demonstrated mechanism.')],['SVT2019','SVTUS2015'])

R['ALS2025']=('AHA adult advanced life support guideline, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001376')
R['REVERT']=('REVERT randomized trial, 2015','https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(15)61485-4')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Unstable Regular Narrow-Complex Tachycardia',
 'An adult develops abrupt regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 210/min, followed immediately by BP 68/40 mmHg, confusion and cool extremities. A pulse is present. Pads are attached and synchronized cardioversion is available. Which next strategy is best?',
 'Perform prompt synchronized cardioversion with concurrent resuscitative support',[
 ('Complete several attempts at vagal maneuvers before preparing cardioversion','Vagal maneuvers are appropriate initial treatment for suitable stable regular SVT. Persistent profound shock and altered mentation attributable to the rhythm make repeated attempts an inappropriate reason to delay cardioversion.'),
 ('Give IV diltiazem and await its full effect before cardioversion','Diltiazem can treat selected stable narrow-complex tachycardias. In severe hypotension it can further compromise pressure, and waiting for drug effect delays definitive treatment of the unstable rhythm.'),
 ('Give a full escalating adenosine sequence before deciding on cardioversion','Adenosine can be considered for regular narrow-complex tachycardia while immediate cardioversion is being arranged. Completing an escalating sequence is not a prerequisite when profound instability persists and cardioversion is ready.')],
 [S('Establish That the Rhythm Is Causing Compromise','**A tachyarrhythmia with a pulse requires urgent synchronized cardioversion when it causes hemodynamic instability.** Hypotension, acute altered mentation, shock, ischemic discomfort or acute HF are clinically important manifestations.',
    'The abrupt rhythm change followed by circulatory deterioration supports causation here. Sinus tachycardia accompanying sepsis or hypovolemia requires treatment of its driver rather than cardioversion simply because the patient is unstable.',
    'A numerical heart-rate threshold is not an independent rule. Assess pulse, rhythm and perfusion together, and support airway, ventilation and circulation while preparing treatment.'),
  S('Deliver a Synchronized Shock Safely','**Synchronization times the shock to ventricular depolarization to reduce the risk of delivering energy during vulnerable repolarization.** Confirm synchronization markers on the QRS and recheck after each attempt because some devices reset the mode.',
    'Use the device-recommended energy or the relevant 2025 rhythm-specific guidance; the AHA 2025 algorithm specifies the maximum energy setting if the recommended setting is unknown. Escalate appropriately if unsuccessful. Provide sedation when feasible, but do not postpone necessary treatment in a rapidly deteriorating patient to complete elective sedation steps.',
    'Adenosine is a possible immediate adjunct for a regular narrow rhythm, not permission to delay available cardioversion through prolonged pharmacological trials. Unsynchronized defibrillation follows a different pathway, including VF or pulseless VT; reassess immediately if the pulse is lost. After conversion, obtain a diagnostic ECG, review reversible causes and document the arrhythmia for definitive evaluation. Successful conversion treats the episode but does not establish the precise reentrant circuit or remove the need to address recurrence.')],['ALS2025','SVT2019'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Modified Valsalva Maneuver for Stable SVT',
 'An alert, hemodynamically stable adult has regular narrow-complex SVT and can cooperate with straining and repositioning. Which vagal maneuver best matches the method supported by the REVERT trial?',
 'A standardized semirecumbent strain followed immediately by supine repositioning and passive leg elevation',[
 ('The same strain while remaining semirecumbent throughout recovery','A standard Valsalva can terminate AV-node-dependent SVT, but remaining semirecumbent omits the postural modification that improved conversion in REVERT.'),
 ('Supine positioning and passive leg elevation without a preceding strain','Passive leg elevation alters venous return but is not the tested modified Valsalva sequence by itself. The standardized strain and immediate recovery-phase positioning are both components of the intervention.'),
 ('A standardized strain followed by standing upright during recovery','Upright recovery does not reproduce the increased venous return of the tested supine leg-raise sequence. It can also provoke presyncope and is not the REVERT intervention.')],
 [S('Use the Tested Sequence','**REVERT used a strain of ==40 mmHg for 15 seconds== from a semirecumbent position, followed immediately by supine repositioning and passive leg elevation.** The legs were raised to about ==45 degrees for 15 seconds== before returning to the semirecumbent position.',
    'The postural change augments venous return during recovery and enhances the vagal response. Use supervised assistance and a practical method of standardizing the strain rather than asking for an unmeasured brief breath hold.',
    'Select a patient who is stable, cooperative and able to perform the maneuver and reposition safely. Shock or severe deterioration calls for the unstable-tachycardia pathway rather than repeated maneuver attempts.'),
  S('Interpret the Trial and the Rhythm Response','**In REVERT, conversion to sinus rhythm occurred in 43% with the modified maneuver versus 17% with the standard maneuver.** The primary endpoint was restoration of sinus rhythm at one minute, not long-term prevention of recurrence.',
    'The intervention was studied in suitable adults with SVT; its findings should not be generalized to AF, flutter or every critically ill patient. AV nodal slowing can reveal atrial activity without actually terminating an atrial tachyarrhythmia.',
    'Record ECG during the attempt and verify the resulting rhythm and perfusion. If an appropriate maneuver fails, monitored adenosine is a usual next option for regular narrow-complex SVT, followed by other treatment according to contraindications and response. Teach a safe self-management plan only after the diagnosis and individual suitability are established. This technique does not require carotid sinus pressure, and carotid massage has a different risk assessment. An initially successful vagal response does not uniquely distinguish AVNRT from AVRT.')],['REVERT','SVT2019','ALS2025'])

R['ADENOLABEL']=('Adenosine rapid-bolus injection prescribing information','https://www.dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=91a601ab-d3cc-4013-9108-1b5432476076')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Adenosine Delivery for Regular Narrow-Complex SVT',
 'A stable adult has regular narrow-complex SVT despite an appropriate vagal maneuver. There is no bronchospasm, transplant history or relevant interacting medication, and a proximal peripheral IV is available. Under the AHA 2025 algorithm, which initial regimen is appropriate?',
 '6 mg rapid IV push followed immediately by saline flush; 12 mg if needed',[
 ('3 mg rapid IV push followed by saline flush; 6 mg if needed','A reduced starting dose may be appropriate in selected circumstances with enhanced adenosine effect or central delivery. Those circumstances are not supplied, and this is not the standard adult peripheral regimen in the named algorithm.'),
 ('6 mg by slow IV injection over several minutes; 12 mg if needed','Adenosine is rapidly removed from the circulation. Slow administration can prevent an effective concentration from reaching the AV node; rapid bolus delivery and immediate flushing are essential to the intended effect.'),
 ('12 mg rapid IV push followed by saline flush as the standard first dose','Twelve milligrams is the usual subsequent dose after an ineffective initial 6-mg bolus in the named algorithm. It should not replace the standard initial dose without a specific justified protocol or circumstance.')],
 [S('Short Duration Requires Rapid Delivery','**The AHA adult algorithm uses ==6 mg rapid IV push== with an immediate saline flush, followed by ==12 mg== if needed.** The rapid-bolus product label describes delivery over ==1–2 seconds== and reassessment before repeat dosing.',
    'Adenosine transiently suppresses AV nodal conduction through adenosine-receptor effects. Rapid cellular uptake and metabolism give it a circulating half-life of less than ten seconds, making line position and technique clinically important.',
    'Use continuous ECG recording, monitoring and resuscitation readiness. Explain the expected brief flushing, chest discomfort or sense of dyspnea while remaining alert to prolonged conduction disturbance or genuine bronchospasm.'),
  S('Reassess the Response Before Escalating','**Termination is common in AV-node-dependent reentry, but a ventricular pause alone is not conversion.** Continuing flutter or atrial tachycardia may become visible during temporary AV block.',
    'Check delivery if no expected effect occurs and review methylxanthine exposure, which can antagonize adenosine. Do not assume that every failure is a resistant reentrant circuit requiring repeated larger doses.',
    'Dipyridamole potentiates adenosine, while carbamazepine can increase AV block; transplantation and central administration can also increase sensitivity and call for a modified approach. Avoid indiscriminate use in irregular or polymorphic wide-complex rhythms and suspected pre-excited AF. Hemodynamic deterioration changes the pathway to prompt electrical treatment. The regimen here is for bolus termination of suitable SVT, not the continuous infusion used in pharmacological stress testing; those indications must not be interchanged.')],['ALS2025','ADENOLABEL'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Adenosine Precautions and Drug Interactions',
 'A stable adult with regular narrow-complex SVT has persistent diffuse wheeze during an acute asthma exacerbation. Vagal maneuvers have failed. Which factor most directly argues against the usual next adenosine bolus?',
 'Active bronchospasm with risk of worsening airflow obstruction',[
 ('Recent caffeine exposure','Caffeine antagonizes adenosine and can reduce efficacy. This interaction affects response assessment but is not the same direct reason to avoid adenosine as active bronchospasm.'),
 ('Concurrent dipyridamole treatment','Dipyridamole potentiates adenosine and may require dose modification or an alternative strategy. It is an important interaction, but the question describes a separate direct respiratory hazard during active bronchospasm.'),
 ('A history of transient flushing during a previous dose','Flushing is a common short-lived pharmacological effect and does not by itself establish a contraindication. It differs from objective bronchospasm or a documented serious hypersensitivity reaction.')],
 [S('Distinguish Expected Dyspnea From Bronchospasm','**Adenosine can provoke clinically important bronchoconstriction and should be avoided in active bronchospasm.** Brief subjective dyspnea after a dose is common, but it does not justify dismissing wheeze or worsening respiratory distress.',
    'The rapid-bolus prescribing information distinguishes bronchospastic disease from obstructive lung disease without bronchoconstriction, where caution is still required. Interpret the current respiratory phenotype rather than relying only on a diagnostic label.',
    'Significant sinus-node dysfunction or second-/third-degree AV block without functioning pacing protection also raises concern because adenosine suppresses nodal activity. Assess the baseline rhythm and available rescue support.'),
  S('Select an Alternative According to the Whole Patient','**Treat the respiratory exacerbation while selecting monitored SVT treatment compatible with pressure and ventricular function.** Verapamil or diltiazem may be considered in suitable stable patients; hypotension or HFrEF can make them inappropriate.',
    'Beta blockade requires particular caution during active bronchospasm. If pharmacological options are contraindicated or ineffective, synchronized cardioversion is an established alternative, and deterioration requires urgent electrical management.',
    'Review interactions before any later adenosine use: methylxanthines antagonize its effect, dipyridamole potentiates it, and carbamazepine can increase conduction block. A previous expected transient sensation is different from a serious adverse reaction. Do not respond to antagonist-associated nonresponse by unbounded dose escalation. Explain the selected treatment and document the actual respiratory findings, because future decisions should distinguish an episode of active bronchospasm from nonspecific discomfort during an otherwise tolerated dose.')],['ADENOLABEL','ALS2025','SVT2019'])

R['DILTLABEL']=('Diltiazem IV prescribing information','https://www.dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=9d51c0f6-26b7-4220-83fb-4e736b289e64')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Alternative Treatment for Persistent Stable Regular SVT',
 'A stable adult has recurrent regular narrow-complex AV-node-dependent SVT despite vagal maneuvers and correctly delivered adenosine. BP is 128/76 mmHg, LVEF is normal, and there is no pre-excitation, conduction disease or recent IV beta blocker. A monitored pharmacological alternative is preferred. Which option is most appropriate?',
 'IV diltiazem with continuous rhythm and blood-pressure monitoring',[
 ('IV digoxin for prompt termination','Digoxin slows AV conduction and has selected rate-control roles, particularly in AF. Its slower and less predictable acute action makes it a poor substitute for rapid termination of this recurrent regular SVT.'),
 ('IV amiodarone as the preferred next nodal treatment','Amiodarone has roles in selected ventricular or atrial arrhythmias and complex substrates. Its broader toxicity and slower action make it less suitable than a standard AV nodal agent for this uncomplicated stable narrow-complex setting.'),
 ('Oral flecainide as the preferred immediate rescue','Flecainide can have selected specialist roles after structural and ischemic assessment. Oral administration is not the usual immediate monitored alternative when an established AV nodal treatment can address the acute episode.')],
 [S('Confirm the Rhythm and the Hemodynamic Margin','**IV diltiazem or verapamil can treat suitable stable regular narrow-complex SVT when vagal maneuvers and adenosine are ineffective or unsuitable.** A beta blocker is another option in an appropriate clinical context.',
    'Review the actual adenosine response and exclude a continuing atrial driver before assuming treatment-resistant AV-node-dependent reentry. Confirm QRS width, regularity and absence of pre-excited AF.',
    'The preserved EF and adequate pressure here support use of a nondihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker. Hypotension, significant systolic dysfunction or an uncertain wide-complex rhythm changes that decision.'),
  S('Dose and Monitor Diltiazem','**The IV label uses ==0.25 mg/kg over 2 minutes== as the initial bolus.** If response is inadequate, a second ==0.35 mg/kg over 2 minutes== can be given after ==15 minutes== according to the prescribing information.',
    'Monitor for hypotension, bradycardia and AV block. Avoid uncritical combination with IV beta blockade because additive nodal and myocardial suppression can be clinically important.',
    'Infusion regimens used for continuing AF rate control should not be assumed necessary after successful conversion of a reentrant SVT. If appropriate medication fails or is contraindicated, synchronized cardioversion is an established alternative even in a currently stable patient. Escalate promptly if perfusion deteriorates. Recurrent symptomatic AVNRT or AVRT warrants discussion of definitive electrophysiological assessment and ablation rather than an indefinite succession of emergency drug trials. Acute response alone does not fully determine the circuit or the best long-term strategy.')],['SVT2019','ALS2025','DILTLABEL'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Uncertain Wide-Complex Tachycardia and AV Nodal Drugs',
 'An adult with prior myocardial infarction has regular monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia at 170/min and BP 112/70 mmHg. The mechanism remains uncertain after initial ECG review. Which proposed approach is least appropriate?',
 'Give IV verapamil as an empirical test for SVT with aberrancy',[
 ('Obtain expert assessment while preparing synchronized cardioversion if deterioration occurs','Current stability permits a focused diagnostic and treatment assessment with rescue readiness. It does not establish a supraventricular mechanism, and cardioversion should be available if the rhythm becomes poorly tolerated.'),
 ('Use an appropriate monitored antiarrhythmic strategy for tolerated monomorphic VT','An uncertain wide-complex rhythm, particularly with prior infarction, warrants a VT-compatible strategy. Drug selection must account for QT, HF, blood pressure and contraindications rather than assuming all agents are equivalent.'),
 ('Consider monitored adenosine if the rhythm remains stable, regular and monomorphic','AHA 2025 permits consideration of adenosine for diagnosis or treatment in this restricted phenotype. It is not permission to use adenosine in unstable, irregular or polymorphic wide-complex tachycardia.')],
 [S('Hemodynamic Tolerance Does Not Exclude VT','**A patient can remain awake with an adequate pressure during ventricular tachycardia.** Previous infarction increases concern for scar-related VT, so stability must not be mistaken for proof of SVT.',
    'Assess a 12-lead ECG, baseline conduction and available rhythm history. AV dissociation, capture or fusion beats support VT, but their absence does not exclude it.',
    'An empirical verapamil challenge can cause severe hypotension or collapse when the rhythm is VT. AHA 2025 advises against verapamil and diltiazem for undifferentiated wide-complex tachycardia.'),
  S('Use a Strategy Compatible With the Uncertainty','**A stable regular monomorphic rhythm can undergo expert evaluation and selected monitored antiarrhythmic treatment.** Synchronized cardioversion is appropriate for instability or when other treatment fails or is unsuitable.',
    'Adenosine may be considered in stable regular monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia to help diagnosis or treat an AV-node-dependent rhythm. Record the response and maintain immediate rescue capability.',
    'Do not extend this exception to irregular wide-complex tachycardia, suspected pre-excited AF or polymorphic VT. Known verapamil-sensitive fascicular VT is a specific specialist diagnosis and does not justify verapamil in an undifferentiated postinfarction rhythm. Distinguish the acute protection decision from later mechanism confirmation; a VT-compatible initial approach reduces the risk of harmful misclassification while additional evidence is obtained. Reassess pulse and perfusion continuously, because loss of pulse requires the cardiac-arrest pathway.')],['ALS2025','SVT2019'])

R['PREG2025']=('ESC cardiovascular disease and pregnancy guideline, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehaf193')
R['AT2025']=('EHRA multisociety atrial tachycardia consensus, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euaf324')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Acute Regular SVT During Pregnancy',
 'At 24 weeks of pregnancy, a patient has abrupt regular narrow-complex SVT at 190/min. BP and perfusion are normal, the heart is structurally normal and an appropriate vagal maneuver has failed. There is no bronchospasm or pre-excitation. Which next treatment is preferred?',
 'Monitored IV adenosine for acute termination',[
 ('IV metoprolol before attempting adenosine','Metoprolol can be used in selected pregnancy-associated SVT when needed, but adenosine is the preferred first medication after failed vagal maneuvers in this suitable stable presentation.'),
 ('IV verapamil before attempting adenosine','Verapamil is an alternative in selected circumstances, with attention to maternal pressure and ventricular function. It is not preferred over adenosine for the supplied uncomplicated stable AV-node-dependent SVT.'),
 ('Immediate catheter ablation as the next acute treatment','Low- or zero-fluoroscopy ablation can be considered for selected recurrent drug-refractory or poorly tolerated arrhythmias during pregnancy. A first suitable drug trial is appropriate for this stable episode rather than proceeding directly to an invasive intervention.')],
 [S('Use a Stability-Based Sequence','**ESC 2025 recommends vagal maneuvers and IV adenosine for suitable hemodynamically stable SVT during pregnancy.** Maternal stabilization benefits fetal perfusion; pregnancy does not justify leaving sustained symptomatic tachycardia untreated.',
    'The ESC pathway uses adenosine boluses within a 6–18-mg framework. Local resuscitation protocols may specify a 6-mg initial dose followed by 12 mg; state the framework rather than implying every organization has an identical upper bolus regimen.',
    'Confirm regularity, QRS width and absence of contraindications. Record the response because AV nodal slowing can reveal an atrial tachyarrhythmia rather than terminate it.'),
  S('Escalate Without Delaying Maternal Resuscitation','**Synchronized cardioversion is indicated when SVT causes hemodynamic instability during pregnancy.** Arrange fetal assessment and appropriate monitoring when feasible, without delaying urgent maternal treatment.',
    'Selected beta blockers, commonly metoprolol, or verapamil may be used when first-line measures fail or are unsuitable. Consider maternal pressure, ventricular function, bronchospasm and fetal effects; atenolol is avoided in the ESC pregnancy framework.',
    'For recurrence, coordinate cardiology, obstetric and anesthesia expertise as appropriate. Balance frequency and tolerance against drug exposure, and consider an experienced low-radiation ablation program for refractory disease. Elective definitive treatment before a planned pregnancy may be preferable in recurrent symptomatic SVT. Do not equate an effective acute bolus with a complete long-term plan, or extrapolate antiarrhythmic safety from nonpregnant adults without checking pregnancy-specific guidance. Reassess reversible triggers and document the rhythm for subsequent decisions.')],['PREG2025','SVT2019'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Incessant Atrial Tachycardia and Arrhythmia-Induced Cardiomyopathy',
 'A patient has near-continuous focal AT at 140/min and new global LV dysfunction with LVEF 30%. Coronary and other initial etiological assessment is unrevealing. Tolerated medical treatment has not suppressed the tachycardia. Which strategy best addresses the suspected reversible driver?',
 'Refer for focal AT mapping and catheter ablation while treating HF and reassessing LV recovery',[
 ('Escalate rate control alone and defer rhythm treatment until EF normalizes','Rate treatment can help selected patients, but persistent tachycardia may itself prevent ventricular recovery. Waiting for EF normalization before addressing the documented driver reverses the necessary treatment sequence.'),
 ('Implant a primary-prevention ICD immediately before attempting rhythm control','An ICD may later be indicated if ventricular dysfunction persists under the relevant criteria. Newly recognized dysfunction with a potentially reversible incessant arrhythmia requires treatment and reassessment rather than assuming an immediate permanent sudden-death indication.'),
 ('Use repeated cardioversion as the preferred durable strategy','Cardioversion may help selected acute atrial tachycardias, but an incessant focal source can recur promptly. Mapping and ablation offer a mechanism-directed durable strategy when the documented rhythm persists despite tolerated treatment.')],
 [S('A Modest Rate Can Still Produce Ventricular Dysfunction','**Arrhythmia-induced cardiomyopathy depends on rhythm burden and duration as well as rate.** An incessant AT at 140/min can be consequential even without extremely rapid paroxysms.',
    'Assess other causes of LV dysfunction and the possibility that pre-existing myocardial disease contributes. Temporal association raises suspicion; improvement after effective rhythm treatment strengthens the attribution.',
    'Document the rhythm burden, ventricular function and clinical HF status. A single office pulse after a brief slower period is insufficient evidence that the driver has been controlled.'),
  S('Eliminate the Driver and Follow Recovery','**ESC SVT guidance recommends ablation for recurrent focal AT, especially when incessant or causing tachycardiomyopathy.** The EHRA multisociety AT consensus also supports ablation for incessant AT with arrhythmia-induced cardiomyopathy.',
    'Use electrophysiological mapping to identify the source and assess anatomical risk. Treat congestion and provide appropriate HF therapy concurrently rather than waiting for ablation to address all hemodynamic consequences.',
    'Reassess symptoms, rhythm burden and EF after treatment. Recovery can take weeks to months and may be incomplete when another cardiomyopathy coexists; avoid promising immediate normalization. Persistent dysfunction requires renewed etiological and device-risk assessment. AV-junction ablation with pacing is a selected fallback when atrial rhythm control is not feasible or effective, not the default substitute for an ablatable focal source. Discuss recurrence and procedural risks, and maintain follow-up even after an initially successful procedure because recurrent tachycardia can again impair ventricular function.')],['SVT2019','AT2025'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Orthodromic and Antidromic AV Reentry',
 'An electrophysiology study demonstrates a regular reentrant tachycardia using antegrade conduction through the AV node and His–Purkinje system, with retrograde return through an atrioventricular accessory pathway. Which classification is correct?',
 'Orthodromic AV reentrant tachycardia',[
 ('Antidromic AV reentrant tachycardia','Antidromic AVRT uses an accessory pathway for antegrade ventricular activation, producing a pre-excited QRS. The antegrade limb here is the normal AV nodal and His–Purkinje route.'),
 ('Typical AV nodal reentrant tachycardia','Typical AVNRT uses functionally distinct nodal pathways rather than requiring an atrioventricular accessory pathway and the ventricle in the circuit. The demonstrated extranodal retrograde limb establishes AVRT.'),
 ('Atrial tachycardia with bystander pre-excitation','An atrial tachycardia can activate the ventricle through a bystander accessory pathway without using it to sustain the atrial rhythm. Here the pathway is an essential retrograde limb of the demonstrated reentrant circuit.')],
 [S('Name the Circuit by Antegrade Activation','**Orthodromic AVRT conducts from atrium to ventricle through the AV node and His–Purkinje system, then returns through an accessory pathway.** Ventricular activation is usually narrow because the normal conduction system activates it.',
    'Antidromic AVRT activates the ventricle antegradely through an accessory pathway and returns through the AV node or another accessory pathway. Its QRS is pre-excited and wide.',
    'QRS width supports the mechanism but is not sufficient alone: orthodromic AVRT can develop bundle-branch aberrancy, while wide-complex tachycardia has several other causes.'),
  S('Distinguish Pathway Participation From Pathway Presence','**A manifest accessory pathway can coexist with a rhythm that does not use it as a necessary circuit limb.** AF, flutter, focal AT or AVNRT can conduct through a bystander pathway.',
    'Electrophysiological maneuvers establish whether pathway conduction is required for the tachycardia. That distinction determines the mechanism and helps select the ablation target.',
    'Acute treatment depends on stability, regularity, QRS pattern and whether pre-excited AF is possible. A nodal-blocking approach suitable for ordinary regular orthodromic AVRT must not be transferred to irregular pre-excited AF. For recurrent symptomatic AVRT, accessory-pathway ablation is a definitive option after discussion of pathway location, procedural risk and patient preferences. A clear circuit description is more informative than using the presence of a delta wave as a general label for every episode of palpitations.')],['SVT2019','SVTUS2015'],style='direct',
 table=(['Mechanism','Antegrade limb','Retrograde limb','Usual QRS'],[
 ['Orthodromic AVRT','AV node–His–Purkinje','Accessory pathway','Narrow unless aberrancy'],
 ['Antidromic AVRT','Accessory pathway','AV node or another pathway','Wide and pre-excited'],
 ['Typical AVNRT','Slow nodal pathway','Fast nodal pathway','Usually narrow']]))

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Concealed Accessory Pathways',
 'A patient has recurrent abrupt regular narrow-complex tachycardia despite a normal PR interval and no delta wave on several sinus ECGs. EPS establishes an accessory pathway that conducts from ventricle to atrium but not antegradely. Which statement is correct?',
 'The pathway can sustain orthodromic AVRT without producing resting ventricular pre-excitation',[
 ('Absence of a delta wave makes the demonstrated pathway incapable of sustaining AVRT','A delta wave requires antegrade pathway conduction. Retrograde conduction can close an orthodromic reentry circuit even when antegrade conduction and resting ventricular activation are normal.'),
 ('The pathway can provide the antegrade limb of antidromic AVRT','The demonstrated pathway has no antegrade conduction. It can return activation to the atrium but cannot itself provide the antegrade pre-excited limb required for antidromic AVRT.'),
 ('The pathway creates the same rapid pre-excited AF mechanism as a short-refractory antegrade pathway','Rapid ventricular pre-excitation during AF requires antegrade accessory conduction. A truly retrograde-only pathway does not supply that mechanism, although AF and other independent risks may still occur.')],
 [S('Concealed Refers to the Direction of Conduction','**A concealed accessory pathway conducts retrogradely and therefore does not produce a delta wave in sinus rhythm.** It can nevertheless participate as the return limb of orthodromic AVRT.',
    'During tachycardia, the AV node and His–Purkinje system activate the ventricle antegradely, and the pathway returns activation to the atrium. The usual narrow QRS does not imply absence of an accessory connection.',
    'Distinguish a proven retrograde-only pathway from intermittent manifest pre-excitation. A normal single ECG in the latter setting may reflect changing competition or pathway conduction rather than permanent absence of antegrade capability.'),
  S('Connect Direction to Risk and Treatment','**The pre-excited AF mechanism of very rapid antegrade pathway conduction does not arise through a genuinely retrograde-only pathway.** This does not mean that every palpitation is benign or that other cardiac risks have been excluded.',
    'Recurrent symptomatic orthodromic AVRT can warrant ablation despite a normal baseline ECG. Mapping establishes location and participation; septal proximity to the normal conduction system affects the procedural discussion.',
    'Selected AV nodal drugs may be appropriate for ordinary orthodromic AVRT when ablation is not chosen, taking account of pressure, ventricular function and contraindications. Do not use the absence of a resting delta wave as the sole reason to give nodal blockade during an undiagnosed irregular wide-complex episode. The direction demonstrated on EPS provides stronger evidence than an isolated surface tracing. Preserve the mechanism in the clinical record so later care distinguishes concealed reentry from manifest pre-excitation and its different emergency implications.')],['SVT2019','SVTUS2015'])

R['AFUS2023']=('ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS AF guideline, 2023','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001193')
R['TACHY2025']=('AHA tachyarrhythmia with pulse algorithm, 2025','https://cpr.heart.org/-/media/CPR-Files/CPR-Guidelines-Files/2025-Algorithms/Algorithm-ACLS-Tachycardia-250514.pdf')
R['IBUTLABEL']=('Ibutilide fumarate prescribing information','https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=0cf8724a-f0d1-4ce2-b7c1-cf5fac345fd0')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Recognition of Pre-Excited Atrial Fibrillation',
 'A young adult has an irregular tachycardia with ventricular rates varying from 180 to 280/min and marked beat-to-beat variation in QRS width and shape. An earlier sinus ECG shows a short PR and delta wave. Which diagnosis best integrates these findings?',
 'Atrial fibrillation with antegrade accessory-pathway conduction',[
 ('Antidromic AV reentrant tachycardia','Antidromic AVRT produces pre-excited wide complexes, but its reentrant cycle is ordinarily regular. The very irregular intervals and changing degrees of pre-excitation favor AF conducting through both available routes.'),
 ('AF with fixed bundle-branch aberrancy','AF with bundle-branch disease can be irregular and wide, but fixed aberrancy generally preserves a more consistent conduction pattern. Known manifest pre-excitation and extreme variable ventricular activation raise concern for accessory conduction.'),
 ('Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia','Polymorphic VT is an important dangerous wide-complex alternative. Here the prior delta wave and irregularly variable pre-excited pattern specifically support AF with accessory conduction, although diagnostic uncertainty must still be managed safely.')],
 [S('Variable Ventricular Activation Signals a Different Emergency','**Pre-excited AF can deliver rapid irregular atrial impulses to the ventricle through an accessory pathway.** Changing competition between AV nodal and accessory conduction produces varying QRS width and morphology.',
    'A prior short PR and delta wave establish manifest pre-excitation as a relevant substrate. The acute tracing must still be interpreted directly; not every wide rhythm in a patient with an accessory pathway is AVRT.',
    'A pathway with a short antegrade refractory period can permit dangerously rapid ventricular responses and degeneration to VF. Assess pulse and perfusion continuously rather than waiting for a particular displayed average rate.'),
  S('Avoid the Ordinary AF Rate-Control Reflex','**AV nodal blocking drugs are contraindicated in pre-excited AF because ventricular acceleration and VF can occur.** The US 2023 guideline includes adenosine, beta blockers, verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin and amiodarone in this warning.',
    'Unstable patients require immediate electrical cardioversion. In suitable stable patients, IV procainamide or ibutilide can be used with monitoring as alternatives to electrical conversion.',
    'If polymorphic VT or VF is present, follow the appropriate unsynchronized-defibrillation pathway; an uncertain rapidly deteriorating wide-complex rhythm must not be exposed to empirical nodal blockade. After stabilization, refer for accessory-pathway assessment and ablation. The crucial distinction is between regular AV-node-dependent reentry, where nodal interruption can terminate the circuit, and irregular antegrade pathway conduction during AF, where that same drug strategy can be hazardous.')],['AFUS2023','SVT2019','ALS2025'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Pharmacological Conversion of Stable Pre-Excited AF',
 'An adult has confirmed pre-excited AF but remains alert with BP 124/78 mmHg and no HF or ischemic discomfort. LV function is preserved, QT is not prolonged and electrolytes are normal. Under US 2023 guidance, which IV drug strategy is appropriate if pharmacological conversion is selected?',
 'Procainamide or ibutilide with continuous monitoring and cardioversion readiness',[
 ('Amiodarone with continuous monitoring','IV amiodarone has roles in AF without pre-excitation and in other arrhythmias. It is contraindicated for pre-excited AF in the named guideline because ventricular acceleration and VF have been reported.'),
 ('Diltiazem with continuous monitoring','Diltiazem can control ventricular rate in ordinary AF when pressure and EF permit. It blocks the AV node without providing the required safe accessory-pathway strategy in pre-excited AF.'),
 ('Adenosine with continuous monitoring','Adenosine can terminate regular AV-node-dependent SVT and has a limited role in stable regular monomorphic wide tachycardia. Irregular pre-excited AF is a different phenotype in which nodal blockade is hazardous.')],
 [S('Select a Pathway-Compatible Drug','**US 2023 recommends IV procainamide or ibutilide for suitable stable pre-excited AF as alternatives to electrical cardioversion.** Stability permits consideration of drugs; it does not make conventional AV nodal rate control safe.',
    'Electrical conversion remains an appropriate option and becomes urgent with attributable hypotension, shock, altered mentation, ischemia or acute HF. Do not prolong a drug infusion while deterioration develops.',
    'Review QT, ventricular function, pressure, electrolytes, renal status and recent antiarrhythmics before selecting treatment. The two agents are alternatives, not a routine sequential combination.'),
  S('Dose With ECG and Hemodynamic Limits','**The AHA wide-QRS algorithm lists procainamide ==20–50 mg/min== to suppression or a stopping limit.** Stop for hypotension, QRS widening greater than 50% or a cumulative ==17 mg/kg==; avoid prolonged QT or significant HF.',
    'Ibutilide labelling uses ==1 mg over 10 minutes== at weight 60 kg or greater, or ==0.01 mg/kg== below 60 kg. If the arrhythmia persists, a second equal 10-minute infusion may begin ==10 minutes after the first infusion ends== when otherwise eligible; stop for conversion, sustained or nonsustained VT, or marked QT/QTc prolongation.',
    'Correct potassium and magnesium and monitor for QT-related polymorphic VT. Continue ECG monitoring for at least four hours after ibutilide or until QTc returns to baseline, longer if arrhythmia occurs. Avoid stacking other QT-prolonging antiarrhythmics without specialist guidance. These are monitored hospital regimens, not unsupervised rescue prescriptions. After conversion, assess stroke prevention as appropriate and arrange definitive pathway treatment; successful acute conversion does not remove the substrate for recurrent rapid pre-excited AF.')],['AFUS2023','TACHY2025','IBUTLABEL'])

R['WPPERISK2024']=('Prospective study of exercise testing and invasive pre-excitation risk assessment, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euae036')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'High-Risk Accessory-Pathway Conduction',
 'During EPS for manifest pre-excitation, induced AF has a shortest pre-excited R–R interval of 220 ms. Which interpretation best matches ESC 2019 risk stratification?',
 'The pathway has a high-risk antegrade conduction property supporting ablation discussion',[
 ('The measurement is reassuring because the average ventricular rate is lower','SPERRI measures the fastest demonstrated pre-excited ventricular conduction rather than the average rate. Longer intervening intervals do not neutralize a documented very short pre-excited interval.'),
 ('Risk cannot be assessed until a spontaneous clinical AF episode is recorded','Induced AF during a properly conducted EPS can assess pathway conduction. A spontaneous dangerous episode is not required before acting on a qualifying high-risk electrophysiological finding.'),
 ('The measurement primarily establishes slow retrograde pathway conduction','The pre-excited R–R interval during AF reflects antegrade delivery to the ventricle. Slowly conducting retrograde pathways are relevant to PJRT but do not explain this rapid antegrade property.')],
 [S('Use the Correct Interval and Threshold','**SPERRI is the shortest pre-excited R–R interval during AF.** ESC 2019 identifies ==SPERRI ≤250 ms== as a high-risk accessory-pathway feature; 220 ms clearly meets that criterion.',
    'Measure genuinely pre-excited complexes and distinguish artefact, fusion and non-pre-excited conduction. A short interval shows that the pathway can deliver closely spaced impulses to the ventricular myocardium.',
    'The pathway effective refractory period is a separate measurement; ==antegrade AP ERP ≤250 ms== is another high-risk feature. The two tests address related conduction properties but are not interchangeable labels.'),
  S('Integrate the Electrophysiological Findings','**Multiple pathways and inducible accessory-pathway-mediated tachycardia also contribute to the ESC high-risk framework.** Catecholamine challenge can expose conduction properties not apparent under baseline conditions.',
    'High-risk findings support catheter ablation after discussion of benefits and procedural risk, including AV-block risk near the septal conduction system. A favorable current symptom history does not erase a documented rapid pathway.',
    'Conversely, a threshold predicts risk rather than guaranteeing VF in an individual. Explain uncertainty without describing observation as equivalent protection against an established high-risk substrate. Assess occupation, athletics, prior syncope and structural disease as part of the overall decision. Do not confuse a retrograde-only concealed pathway with a manifest pathway capable of rapid antegrade AF conduction. After successful ablation, verify pathway elimination and arrange follow-up appropriate to symptoms and procedural findings.')],['SVT2019'],
 table=(['EPS finding','ESC 2019 high-risk feature'],[
 ['Shortest pre-excited RR during AF','≤250 ms'],
 ['Antegrade accessory-pathway ERP','≤250 ms'],
 ['Pathway number','Multiple accessory pathways'],
 ['Inducibility','Accessory-pathway-mediated tachycardia at baseline or catecholamine challenge']]))

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Asymptomatic Pre-Excitation in a High-Risk Occupation',
 'An asymptomatic commercial pilot has manifest ventricular pre-excitation. During exercise the delta wave gradually becomes less obvious, without an abrupt documented transition to fully normal conduction. Which next approach best matches ESC 2019 guidance?',
 'Refer for EPS-based pathway risk stratification, including appropriate catecholamine assessment',[
 ('Declare the pathway low risk because pre-excitation becomes less visible with exercise','Gradual reduction can reflect faster AV nodal conduction and changing fusion rather than actual pathway block. It is not equivalent to abrupt complete loss of pre-excitation and does not settle risk in this occupation.'),
 ('Use ambulatory monitoring alone and investigate further only after symptoms occur','Monitoring can contribute to noninvasive assessment, but high-risk occupations warrant invasive risk stratification under the named framework. Waiting for symptoms may leave an important occupational hazard unassessed.'),
 ('Proceed to ablation without characterizing pathway properties or location','Ablation can be indicated when high-risk properties are demonstrated and can be considered in other selected circumstances. Characterization and discussion of location-specific procedural risk remain necessary rather than treating every asymptomatic delta wave identically.')],
 [S('Occupational Consequences Change the Evaluation','**ESC 2019 recommends EPS risk stratification for asymptomatic pre-excitation in high-risk occupations and competitive athletes.** The clinical concern includes both pathway-mediated sudden-death risk and the consequences of an arrhythmic event during the activity.',
    'Assess prior overlooked palpitations or syncope, family and structural history, and the actual ECG evidence. Asymptomatic means no recognized clinical event, not proof of a pathway incapable of rapid conduction.',
    'EPS evaluates antegrade refractoriness, pre-excited AF conduction, inducible AVRT and multiple pathways, with catecholamine assessment when appropriate.'),
  S('Understand the Limits of Exercise Reassurance','**Abrupt complete loss of pre-excitation suggests less robust antegrade pathway conduction, but gradual fading is not the same observation.** Increasing AV nodal contribution can obscure a delta wave while pathway conduction persists.',
    'Intermittent pre-excitation and a reassuring exercise result do not guarantee absence of high-risk invasive properties. A prospective 2024 study found limited sensitivity of low-risk exercise findings for excluding short SPERRI or AP ERP.',
    'Discuss ablation when EPS identifies high-risk properties and weigh septal AV-block risk, patient preference and applicable occupational assessment. Selected genuinely low-risk patients can be followed, but the decision should follow an adequate evaluation rather than the visual disappearance of a small delta wave at peak exercise. Do not portray noninvasive testing as useless; it supplies evidence whose limitations matter when the consequence of a missed high-risk pathway is substantial.')],['SVT2019','WPPERISK2024'])

R['AVNRTENDPOINT']=('Multicenter study of slow-pathway ablation endpoints, 2019','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacep.2018.09.012')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Slow-Pathway Ablation for Recurrent AVNRT',
 'A patient has recurrent symptomatic typical AVNRT requiring emergency treatment despite an acceptable trial of medication. EPS confirms the mechanism, and the patient chooses definitive treatment. Which ablation target is generally preferred?',
 'The AV nodal slow-pathway region with preservation of normal AV conduction',[
 ('The AV nodal fast-pathway region as the routine first target','Fast-pathway ablation can interrupt nodal reentry but has a greater risk of damaging essential AV conduction. Contemporary routine treatment targets the slow pathway rather than sacrificing the fast pathway.'),
 ('An atrioventricular accessory pathway at the earliest retrograde atrial site','Accessory-pathway ablation treats AVRT when pathway participation is established. An early retrograde atrial signal during confirmed AVNRT does not by itself establish an extranodal accessory connection.'),
 ('The compact AV node followed by permanent pacing','AV-junction ablation with pacing has selected roles in refractory rate control, especially atrial arrhythmias. It intentionally creates dependence on pacing and is disproportionate when slow-pathway modification can treat AVNRT while preserving conduction.')],
 [S('Treat the Demonstrated Reentrant Substrate','**Slow-pathway modification is the usual ablation strategy for symptomatic recurrent AVNRT.** It interrupts the substrate for reentry while preserving clinically adequate antegrade AV conduction.',
    'Typical slow–fast AVNRT uses slow antegrade and fast retrograde nodal conduction. The anatomy is approached through inferior nodal extensions rather than treating every early atrial signal as an accessory pathway.',
    'Ablation offers high success, but procedural risk is not zero. Discuss AV block requiring pacing, vascular complications, recurrence and the alternatives in terms appropriate to the patient and center.'),
  S('Use an Appropriate Procedural Endpoint','**Noninducibility of the clinical AVNRT is an important success endpoint.** A junctional rhythm during RF delivery is a useful procedural observation but does not alone prove durable success.',
    'Residual dual nodal physiology does not automatically justify progressively hazardous lesions when the clinical arrhythmia is no longer inducible. The multicenter endpoint study cautions against unnecessary attempts to abolish every residual nodal feature.',
    'Cryoablation can reduce the risk of permanent AV injury in selected settings but may carry more recurrence than RF; energy choice depends on anatomy and operator assessment. Observation remains reasonable for selected minimally symptomatic patients with rare brief episodes, while nodal drugs are options when ablation is unwanted or unsuitable. The recurrent emergency-treated presentation here supports definitive treatment. Follow symptoms after the procedure because palpitations can reflect ectopy or another rhythm rather than recurrent AVNRT; document the rhythm before assuming procedural failure.')],['SVT2019','SVTUS2015','AVNRTENDPOINT'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Accessory-Pathway Ablation Near the Normal Conduction System',
 'A patient with recurrent symptomatic AVRT chooses ablation. Mapping locates an anteroseptal accessory pathway close to His recordings. Which counseling statement is most appropriate?',
 'Ablation can be effective, but proximity to normal conduction increases AV-block risk and affects energy and strategy selection',[
 ('The AV-block risk is the same as for a left lateral free-wall pathway','All procedures require individualized risk assessment, but septal proximity to the AV node and His system adds a specific conduction-injury concern that is not equivalent to a distant free-wall target.'),
 ('Cryoablation is generally chosen for lower recurrence than radiofrequency ablation','Cryomapping and cryoenergy can improve the safety margin near vulnerable conduction tissue. Their attraction here is protection of normal conduction; recurrence may be higher than with radiofrequency energy, so lower recurrence is not the general rationale for choosing cryoablation.'),
 ('Successful tachycardia termination alone proves complete pathway elimination','Termination is encouraging but can occur for reasons other than permanent pathway block. Assess the relevant antegrade and retrograde conduction and noninducibility, including appropriate waiting or provocative assessment.')],
 [S('Location Changes the Procedural Balance','**Accessory-pathway ablation is a definitive treatment for recurrent symptomatic AVRT.** For anteroseptal and midseptal locations, normal AV conduction tissue may be close to the required lesion.',
    'Explain the possibility of AV block and permanent pacing alongside the expected benefit of eliminating pathway-mediated arrhythmia. Do not apply a single universal complication percentage across locations, techniques and centers.',
    'Detailed mapping and an experienced operator help define a safe strategy. Cryoenergy may be considered near the conduction system because reversible cryomapping and tissue adherence offer useful procedural features.'),
  S('Verify the Intended Result','**Success requires assessment of pathway conduction, not merely a slower pulse or one terminated episode.** Test the relevant conduction direction and ability to induce the clinical arrhythmia using the appropriate electrophysiological protocol.',
    'A manifest pathway requires attention to antegrade pre-excitation as well as any retrograde function. A concealed pathway has no delta wave to use as the sole endpoint, so retrograde pathway behavior is particularly important.',
    'Cryoablation can carry a higher recurrence burden than RF in some settings, and no energy source removes all risk. Discuss alternatives, including medication or observation when clinically suitable, without minimizing a separately demonstrated high-risk antegrade pathway. After the procedure, evaluate recurrent symptoms with ECG evidence and reassess any return of pre-excitation. Distinguish pathway ablation from AV-junction ablation: the aim is to remove the abnormal connection while retaining normal conduction, not deliberately create complete block.')],['SVT2019','SVTUS2015'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Cavotricuspid-Isthmus-Dependent Atrial Flutter',
 'EPS confirms a right atrial macroreentrant circuit rotating around the tricuspid annulus and requiring conduction through the isthmus between that annulus and the inferior vena cava. Which lesion target directly interrupts the essential circuit?',
 'A cavotricuspid isthmus line with confirmed bidirectional conduction block',[
 ('Pulmonary vein isolation as the sole lesion set','Pulmonary vein isolation is the principal lesion strategy for AF. It does not directly interrupt the demonstrated right atrial isthmus-dependent flutter circuit, although AF may coexist and require separate treatment.'),
 ('A mitral isthmus line','A mitral isthmus line can treat selected left atrial perimitral macroreentry when mapping supports that circuit. The demonstrated circuit here is around the tricuspid annulus in the right atrium.'),
 ('AV nodal slow-pathway modification','Slow-pathway modification treats AVNRT. The AV node affects ventricular conduction during flutter but is not the essential limb of the described atrial macroreentrant circuit.')],
 [S('Define Typical Flutter by Circuit Dependence','**Typical flutter is a right atrial macroreentrant rhythm dependent on the cavotricuspid isthmus.** Both counterclockwise and clockwise rotation can use this critical region.',
    'Counterclockwise flutter often produces negative inferior flutter waves and positive activity in V1. Clockwise rotation can alter polarity; scar, surgery and prior ablation can make surface appearances misleading.',
    'The atrial circuit can continue despite AV block because ventricular activation is not required to sustain it. This distinguishes flutter from ordinary AVRT, whose circuit includes the ventricle.'),
  S('Verify a Complete Functional Line','**The procedural endpoint is bidirectional CTI block, not tachycardia termination alone.** Residual slow conduction through a gap can permit recurrence even after the presenting episode stops.',
    'Use pacing and electrophysiological assessment to demonstrate block in both directions, with an appropriate reassessment period. Do not infer complete block simply from a change in surface flutter-wave appearance.',
    'Recurrent symptomatic typical flutter is well suited to CTI ablation after review of risks and thromboembolic protection. Acute instability calls for immediate cardioversion rather than waiting for an elective procedure. CTI treatment addresses this circuit but does not erase the atrial substrate for AF or determine whether anticoagulation can later stop. Atypical flutter requires mapping of its own circuit rather than empirical repetition of a CTI line. Explain the difference between eliminating an atrial circuit and merely slowing its ventricular expression with nodal drugs.')],['SVT2019','AFUS2023','AT2025'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Anticoagulation After Successful Typical Flutter Ablation',
 'A 74-year-old with hypertension, diabetes and previously documented AF undergoes successful CTI ablation for recurrent typical flutter. Six weeks later, the patient is asymptomatic in sinus rhythm without bleeding or another anticoagulation contraindication. Which strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Continue anticoagulation according to the established AF stroke-risk indication',[
 ('Stop anticoagulation because bidirectional CTI block was achieved','Bidirectional block demonstrates treatment of the flutter circuit. It does not eliminate prior AF or the patient’s stroke-risk factors, so procedural success alone is not a reason to withdraw indicated anticoagulation.'),
 ('Replace anticoagulation with aspirin because flutter has not recurred','Aspirin is not an equivalent substitute for oral anticoagulation when AF-related stroke risk warrants treatment. Freedom from recognized flutter does not change that comparison.'),
 ('Suspend anticoagulation unless a future short monitor captures recurrent AF','Intermittent monitoring can miss silent AF and is useful for surveillance, but the prior established AF diagnosis and high stroke risk already support continued treatment. A new recording is not required to re-establish the indication.')],
 [S('Separate Flutter Cure From Stroke Prevention','**CTI ablation eliminates an isthmus-dependent circuit but does not cure all atrial arrhythmia susceptibility.** AF may precede flutter, coexist with it or emerge after successful ablation.',
    'US 2023 guidance recommends ongoing anticoagulation after successful CTI ablation in patients with previously detected AF when indicated by the AF risk profile. This patient has multiple non-sex stroke-risk factors under either major contemporary framework.',
    'Symptom relief is not proof of absence of AF. Silent recurrence and changing comorbidity burden are reasons for continued clinical reassessment rather than treatment withdrawal based on pulse regularity.'),
  S('Distinguish the Early Procedural Minimum From Long-Term Need','**After successful flutter cardioversion or ablation, US 2023 guidance recommends anticoagulation for at least ==4 weeks==.** That early minimum is not an automatic stop date for a patient with an ongoing indication.',
    'When no prior AF is known, long-term decisions require stroke-risk assessment and attention to future AF risk. High-risk patients who are not anticoagulated need appropriate surveillance; selected high-risk profiles can justify long-term treatment after discussion.',
    'Review renal function, dosing, interactions and modifiable bleeding risks during follow-up. Continued anticoagulation should be prescribed correctly rather than retained without periodic reassessment. A major bleeding contraindication would require another individualized strategy, but it is not supplied here. Explain that rhythm treatment and embolic-risk treatment have different targets, so a successful procedure can coexist with a continuing need for stroke prevention.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Atypical Scar-Related Atrial Flutter',
 'Months after left atrial AF ablation, a patient develops sustained organized atrial tachycardia. The surface waves differ from classic inferior saw-tooth flutter. Prior CTI block remains intact. Which approach best defines the substrate before repeat ablation?',
 'Activation and entrainment assessment to identify the actual atrial circuit and critical conduction channel',[
 ('Repeat the CTI line solely because the rhythm is called flutter','A CTI line treats a demonstrated isthmus-dependent circuit. Intact block and a post-left-atrial-ablation substrate raise other possibilities; the generic flutter label does not establish a CTI gap.'),
 ('Select a focal ablation target solely because an isoelectric interval is visible','A discrete-looking surface pattern can occur with scar-related macroreentry. An isoelectric interval does not prove focal origin when atrial activation is altered by prior lesions.'),
 ('Create a mitral isthmus line solely from the history of left atrial ablation','Perimitral reentry is plausible, but roof-dependent or other scar-related circuits can also occur. An empirical line without circuit definition may fail to address the rhythm and add further proarrhythmic gaps.')],
 [S('Atypical Flutter Describes a Different Circuit Family','**Non-CTI-dependent macroreentrant AT can use anatomical barriers, surgical scars or prior ablation lesions.** Left atrial examples include perimitral and roof-dependent circuits.',
    'Prior ablation can create slow-conduction channels or incomplete lines that permit organized reentry. The arrhythmia may appear after a period of sinus rhythm and need not resemble the original AF.',
    'Surface morphology is less reliable after atrial scarring. Atypical wave appearance does not exclude a right atrial circuit, and a visually discrete P wave does not prove a nonreentrant focal mechanism.'),
  S('Map Before Adding Lesions','**Activation mapping and appropriate entrainment maneuvers identify circuit participation and a critical isthmus.** Integrate local signals with the full tachycardia cycle rather than ablating the earliest visible deflection without context.',
    'Treat a mapped channel or complete an indicated line, then verify its intended conduction endpoint. Termination alone can be misleading if a gap remains or the rhythm changes to another circuit.',
    'Assess symptoms, ventricular response, LV function and anticoagulation before intervention. Cardioversion is available for acute compromise or selected symptom relief, but recurrent scar-related tachycardia often requires a mechanism-based durable plan. Discuss that repeat atrial procedures can be more complex than a first CTI ablation and can uncover multiple circuits. Avoid applying a single ECG label as a procedural map; the anatomy and electrophysiology determine which lesion is useful.')],['AT2025','SVT2019','AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'ECG Confirmation of Suspected Atrial Fibrillation',
 'A smartwatch using photoplethysmography repeatedly reports an irregular pulse in an asymptomatic adult. No ECG tracing has been recorded. Which next step is most appropriate before establishing a clinical AF diagnosis?',
 'Obtain an interpretable ECG recording and have the rhythm reviewed by a qualified clinician',[
 ('Diagnose AF from repeated optical alerts because repetition removes false positives','Repeated alerts increase the reason to investigate but do not convert pulse sensing into atrial electrical documentation. Ectopy and artefact can generate recurrent optical irregularity.'),
 ('Use the absence of palpitations to dismiss the alerts','AF can be asymptomatic, so symptom absence cannot exclude it. The appropriate response is proportionate confirmatory recording rather than either immediate labelling or dismissal.'),
 ('Use left atrial enlargement on echocardiography as confirmation','Atrial enlargement can identify substrate and influence management, but it is not rhythm documentation. Echo cannot establish that an optical alert represented AF rather than another irregular rhythm.')],
 [S('An Optical Pulse Signal Is a Screening Clue','**Clinical AF requires ECG documentation rather than photoplethysmographic irregularity alone.** A wearable that records an actual ECG is different from one that supplies only an optical pulse alert.',
    'Review for irregular ventricular intervals and absence of a consistent repeating P-wave sequence, recognizing that pacing or AV conduction disease can alter the ventricular pattern. A clinician should inspect the tracing rather than accept an automated label uncritically.',
    'Choose a 12-lead ECG if the rhythm is present, or an appropriate ambulatory or patient-recorded ECG strategy when episodes are intermittent. A normal symptom-free snapshot does not exclude paroxysmal AF.'),
  S('Interpret Recording Duration in Context','**ESC 2024 recognizes AF across a conventional 12-lead recording and interpretable single- or multilead ECG devices.** A conventional 12-lead ECG is usually ten seconds; it need not be extended to thirty seconds to count as that recording.',
    'Thirty seconds or longer has generally been the consensus convention for ambulatory single- or multilead episodes, but the evidence for a universal minimum is limited and the clinical context matters. Do not turn an arbitrary duration into proof of treatment benefit.',
    'Brief device-detected atrial episodes require confirmation and an assessment distinct from automatically treating every alert as established clinical AF. Once AF is confirmed, evaluate comorbidity, stroke risk and rate or rhythm needs independently of symptoms. If the tracing instead shows ectopy, flutter or artefact, address that actual finding. This sequence avoids both missing silent AF and exposing a patient to diagnosis-specific treatment without a verified rhythm.')],['AF2024','AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Long-Standing Persistent Versus Permanent AF',
 'A patient has continuously documented AF for 16 months. The patient and treating team are actively considering cardioversion and ablation to restore sinus rhythm. Under ESC 2024 terminology, which classification best fits?',
 'Long-standing persistent AF',[
 ('Permanent AF','Permanent AF describes a shared decision that no further restoration attempts are planned. Duration alone does not establish that treatment decision, and rhythm restoration remains under consideration here.'),
 ('Paroxysmal AF','Paroxysmal AF terminates within the short-episode framework, conventionally within seven days. Continuous AF for sixteen months does not fit a self-terminating episodic pattern.'),
 ('First-diagnosed AF as the only applicable description','First-diagnosed refers to initial recognition irrespective of duration and symptoms. The documented continuous sixteen-month history supports a more specific temporal classification rather than limiting the description to first recognition.')],
 [S('Duration and Therapeutic Intent Describe Different Things','**Long-standing persistent AF is continuous AF for at least ==12 months== when rhythm control remains an option.** The twelve-month boundary is a practical classification convention, not a point beyond which restoration becomes impossible.',
    'Permanent AF reflects an agreed management strategy of no further planned attempts to restore sinus rhythm. A short or long history can lead to that decision, and it can be revisited if circumstances and preferences change.',
    'Document what is known from monitoring and clinical history. Symptom duration may underestimate rhythm duration because AF can be silent.'),
  S('Do Not Let the Label Replace Clinical Decisions','**Temporal classification helps communicate rhythm behavior but does not independently determine anticoagulation or ablation eligibility.** Stroke prevention follows the appropriate risk assessment rather than the word paroxysmal or persistent alone.',
    'Longer continuous AF can indicate a more advanced atrial substrate and affect rhythm-control expectations. Assess atrial size, comorbidity, symptoms, ventricular function and prior treatment before counseling about likely benefit.',
    'A patient initially managed as permanent AF can later reconsider rhythm control through shared decision-making; the label describes intent rather than irreversible biological proof. Conversely, classifying an episode as paroxysmal does not make embolic risk negligible. Record the duration pattern and management strategy separately so later care does not inherit an unsupported assumption about either risk or treatment feasibility.')],['AF2024','AFUS2023'],
 table=(['Term','Practical meaning'],[
 ['First-diagnosed','First recognition, regardless of duration or symptoms'],
 ['Paroxysmal','Terminates within 7 days, spontaneously or with intervention'],
 ['Persistent','Non-self-terminating AF; many intervention trials use >7 days as the duration cut-off'],
 ['Long-standing persistent','Continuous ≥12 months, with rhythm control still an option'],
 ['Permanent','Shared decision: no further planned sinus-restoration attempts']]))

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Initial Evaluation of Newly Diagnosed AF',
 'A stable adult has newly ECG-confirmed AF without chest pain, hypoxemia, signs of venous thromboembolism or another acute warning feature. Which initial investigation strategy best guides management?',
 'Transthoracic echo, blood count, metabolic and thyroid testing, with additional tests directed by clinical findings',[
 ('Routine coronary angiography and CT pulmonary angiography to identify the cause','Coronary and pulmonary vascular investigations are appropriate when symptoms or findings suggest those diagnoses. AF alone does not justify protocolized invasive coronary or pulmonary embolism testing in this presentation.'),
 ('Transesophageal echo instead of a transthoracic and laboratory assessment','TEE is valuable for selected atrial-thrombus questions, particularly before some cardioversions. It does not replace the broader initial assessment of ventricular structure, valves and reversible systemic contributors.'),
 ('Ambulatory ECG alone before any structural or laboratory evaluation','Monitoring can quantify burden or correlate symptoms after diagnosis. It does not provide the ventricular, valvular, renal or thyroid information needed to select safe AF treatment.')],
 [S('Define the Substrate and Treatment Constraints','**US 2023 recommends transthoracic echocardiography and blood count, metabolic and thyroid assessment for newly diagnosed AF.** The tests identify contributors and information that changes treatment choices.',
    'Echo evaluates LV function, chamber size and important valve disease. Significant mitral stenosis, systolic dysfunction or another structural abnormality can alter anticoagulant, rate-control and rhythm-drug selection.',
    'Laboratory assessment identifies anemia, electrolyte abnormalities, renal dysfunction and thyroid disease. Use clinical context to extend testing rather than treating the initial panel as an exhaustive etiological screen.'),
  S('Investigate Specific Suspicions Rather Than the AF Label','**Do not routinely screen every new AF patient for ACS, ischemia or PE without additional supporting signs or symptoms.** The absence of a routine indication differs from ignoring a clinically suspected emergency.',
    'Evaluate pressure, oxygenation, symptoms, HF, alcohol exposure, medications and relevant comorbidities. Review sleep-disordered breathing and cardiometabolic risks as part of the ongoing assessment.',
    'TEE answers a different question from routine TTE: it can assess atrial thrombus when early cardioversion or another procedure makes that consequential. A normal TTE does not reliably exclude left atrial appendage thrombus. Begin stroke-risk and symptom assessment while investigations proceed; the diagnostic work-up should support rather than indefinitely postpone indicated care. Reassess results before prescribing agents whose safety depends on EF, renal function, QT or structural substrate. Document the reason for targeted advanced testing so AF is not used as an automatic explanation for every symptom.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Comorbidity Treatment in the AF-CARE Pathway',
 'An overweight patient with symptomatic paroxysmal AF has poorly controlled hypertension, low fitness and regular alcohol intake. Rhythm-control treatment is being planned. Which additional strategy best follows ESC 2024 AF-CARE principles?',
 'Treat blood pressure and weight, prescribe tailored exercise and reduce alcohol alongside stroke and rhythm management',[
 ('Defer risk-factor treatment until the rhythm-control procedure has been assessed','Ablation or drug treatment can improve rhythm, but persistent comorbid drivers can undermine the response. Risk-factor care is a concurrent treatment component rather than a step reserved for procedural failure.'),
 ('Use risk-factor treatment as a substitute for indicated anticoagulation','Lifestyle and comorbidity treatment can reduce AF burden and improve health, but they do not replace anticoagulation when embolic risk warrants it. Stroke prevention requires its own assessment.'),
 ('Choose broad exercise restriction to reduce adrenergic triggers','Selected activity precautions may be needed for an individual disorder, but a tailored exercise program improves fitness and can reduce AF recurrence. Routine deconditioning is not the preferred strategy for this phenotype.')],
 [S('Treat the Substrate Alongside the Arrhythmia','**AF-CARE begins with comorbidity and risk-factor management.** The remaining components address avoidance of stroke, reduction of symptoms through rate or rhythm control, and repeated evaluation.',
    'Treat hypertension, HF and diabetes appropriately and assess obesity, inactivity, alcohol exposure and sleep-disordered breathing. These factors influence AF progression and the success of rhythm treatment.',
    'The plan should be individualized and practical, with follow-up of adherence and clinical response rather than a one-time list of lifestyle instructions.'),
  S('Use Specific Goals Without Overpromising','**ESC 2024 recommends a weight-loss target of ==10% or more== in overweight or obese individuals with AF.** A tailored exercise program is recommended to improve fitness and reduce recurrence.',
    'The ESC alcohol target is ==no more than 3 standard drinks or 30 g alcohol per week==. Standard drink sizes vary internationally, so grams clarify the intended limit; abstinence can be an appropriate individual goal.',
    'Evaluate and treat sleep apnea according to the respiratory diagnosis, while recognizing that evidence for guaranteed AF prevention from apnea treatment is less certain than treating its established respiratory indications. Coordinate these measures with anticoagulation and symptom treatment rather than delaying necessary care until a weight target is reached. Reassess risk factors over time because new age-related or medical risks can change stroke prevention and drug selection. Improvement in AF burden is valuable but does not by itself prove that embolic risk has disappeared.')],['AF2024','AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Follow-Up After Trigger-Induced AF',
 'A patient has a first ECG-documented AF episode during pneumonia and sepsis. Sinus rhythm returns as the infection resolves. At discharge, the patient is stable and has hypertension and diabetes. Which follow-up plan is most appropriate?',
 'Arrange AF surveillance and reassess thromboembolic risk and anticoagulation after recovery',[
 ('Discharge without rhythm follow-up because the infection explains the episode','An acute trigger can initiate AF in a susceptible substrate, and recurrence after medical illness is common. Resolution of the infection does not establish permanent freedom from AF or remove the need to assess stroke risk.'),
 ('Require recurrent symptomatic AF before assessing anticoagulation','AF may recur silently, and a verified episode already supplies a reason for risk assessment. Waiting for symptoms can miss recurrent disease and does not reflect contemporary trigger-induced AF guidance.'),
 ('Prescribe indefinite anticoagulation using the sepsis-associated episode alone as the indication','Long-term anticoagulation can be appropriate after risk assessment, but the trigger alone is not a complete prescription rule. Acute illness, bleeding risk, renal recovery and expected net benefit must be considered.')],
 [S('A Reversible Trigger Does Not Prove a Reversible Substrate','**AF identified during acute illness has a substantial subsequent recurrence risk.** Infection, inflammation, adrenergic stress and electrolyte changes can reveal underlying atrial susceptibility.',
    'US 2023 guidance supports outpatient follow-up for AF surveillance and thromboembolic decision-making. ESC 2024 applies AF-CARE principles to trigger-induced AF after diagnostic confirmation.',
    'Explain recurrence symptoms and arrange an appropriate recording strategy if needed. An asymptomatic regular pulse at discharge is not adequate long-term surveillance by itself.'),
  S('Separate Acute Sepsis Decisions From Long-Term Prevention','**The benefit of anticoagulation for stroke prevention during critical sepsis is uncertain.** Do not equate routine venous thromboprophylaxis with therapeutic AF anticoagulation or automatically extrapolate stable-outpatient evidence to active critical illness.',
    'After the trigger is corrected, consider long-term oral anticoagulation in suitable patients with elevated thromboembolic risk, balancing bleeding, renal function, drug interactions and preferences. The recommendation is not that every brief sepsis-associated episode demands identical treatment.',
    'Review pressure, diabetes, weight, HF and other relevant conditions, and reassess AF burden and stroke risk dynamically. A recurrent episode may change symptom or rhythm-control needs even when prevention has already been addressed. Document the original ECG and clinical circumstances so future clinicians do not dismiss the diagnosis as an unverified monitor label. Distinguish accepted follow-up recommendations from the remaining evidence gap: dedicated randomized trials of anticoagulation specifically for trigger-induced AF are limited.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'ESC 2024 Stroke-Risk Assessment in AF',
 'A 67-year-old woman has ECG-confirmed paroxysmal AF and treated hypertension. She has no HF, diabetes, vascular disease, prior embolism or major bleeding contraindication. Under the ESC 2024 CHA2DS2-VA framework, which interpretation is correct?',
 'Score 2: oral anticoagulation is recommended',[
 ('Score 1: anticoagulation is only a consideration','Age 65–74 contributes one point and hypertension another, giving two non-sex points. Omitting either factor understates the score and changes the recommendation.'),
 ('Score 3: female sex supplies the third CHA2DS2-VA point','The older CHA2DS2-VASc scheme includes sex category, but CHA2DS2-VA deliberately omits that component. The treatment conclusion may still favor anticoagulation, but the requested score is two.'),
 ('Score 2: anticoagulation is deferred unless AF becomes persistent','The score is correct, but clinical AF stroke prevention does not require progression to persistent AF. Paroxysmal episodes do not negate an established risk-based indication.')],
 [S('Count Non-Sex Risk Components','**CHA2DS2-VA omits sex category while retaining the other familiar stroke-risk components.** This patient receives one point for age 65–74 and one for hypertension.',
    'Age at least 75 receives two points instead of the younger age point; prior stroke, TIA or arterial thromboembolism receives two. Do not count mutually exclusive age categories together.',
    'HF, hypertension, diabetes and vascular disease supply one point each according to their guideline definitions. Controlled hypertension still counts as a history of hypertension.'),
  S('Apply the Named Threshold and Reassess','**ESC 2024 recommends anticoagulation at ==CHA2DS2-VA ≥2== and advises considering it at ==1==.** A score of zero generally identifies low risk, while disease-specific exceptions require separate assessment.',
    'AF with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy or cardiac amyloidosis has recommendations that do not depend solely on a low score. Mechanical valves and significant mitral stenosis also change anticoagulant selection.',
    'Choose an appropriate agent and dose after reviewing renal function, interactions and bleeding risks. Reassess as age and comorbidities change; the score is not a one-time clearance. Avoid using symptoms, a short observed episode or apparent rhythm success as substitutes for embolic-risk assessment. Distinguish this ESC framework from US sex-specific score examples so a question does not silently switch systems. Discuss benefits and risks in patient-centered terms without presenting any score as a perfectly calibrated individual prediction.')],['AF2024'],
 table=(['CHA2DS2-VA component','Points'],[
 ['Chronic HF','1'],['Hypertension','1'],['Age ≥75 years','2'],['Diabetes','1'],
 ['Prior stroke/TIA/arterial thromboembolism','2'],['Vascular disease','1'],['Age 65–74 years','1'],['Sex category','Not included']]))

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'US 2023 Intermediate Stroke-Risk Threshold',
 'A 61-year-old man has clinical AF and hypertension as his only CHA2DS2-VASc factor. No disease-specific high-risk condition or anticoagulation contraindication is present. Under US 2023 guidance, which statement best describes the prevention decision?',
 'Anticoagulation is reasonable after individualized discussion at this intermediate-risk level',[
 ('Anticoagulation is not considered until another score point develops','The guideline considers anticoagulation reasonable at an estimated annual risk of at least 1% but below 2%, exemplified by a score of one in men. A second point is not a prerequisite for discussing treatment.'),
 ('Aspirin is the preferred alternative because the score is only one','Aspirin is not an equivalent substitute for anticoagulation for AF stroke prevention. An intermediate-risk decision requires assessment of anticoagulation benefit rather than automatic antiplatelet substitution.'),
 ('The patient meets the high-risk class I score example of at least two points in men','Hypertension supplies one point, and age below 65 supplies none. This is the intermediate category in the named framework rather than the higher-risk score example.')],
 [S('Anchor the Recommendation to Estimated Risk','**US 2023 recommends anticoagulation at estimated annual thromboembolic risk ==≥2%==, with score examples of ==≥2 in men or ≥3 in women==.** At ==≥1% but <2%==, anticoagulation is reasonable.',
    'The intermediate examples are CHA2DS2-VASc one in men or two in women. Female sex acts as a risk modifier in interpretation; sex category alone should not be treated like multiple non-sex risk factors.',
    'Population calibration varies, and the point score is not a guarantee of an exact individual annual percentage. Apply clinical context and any condition-specific recommendation.'),
  S('Resolve Intermediate-Risk Uncertainty','**Use shared decision-making to assess the likely net benefit.** Review AF burden and other relevant risk modifiers when the patient remains uncertain, together with bleeding, renal function, interactions and preferences.',
    'Correct modifiable bleeding risks rather than treating a bleeding score alone as a prohibition. A selected DOAC is usually preferred when eligible, while mechanical valves or significant rheumatic mitral stenosis require a different agent framework.',
    'Do not postpone reassessment indefinitely after an initial decision against treatment; new diabetes, age progression or another risk factor can change the balance. Paroxysmal rhythm and absent symptoms do not make the decision irrelevant. Explain why reasonable at intermediate risk differs from a universal mandatory rule, and why aspirin is not the default middle option. Document the framework and factors considered so subsequent care can revisit a clear decision rather than inherit an unexplained prescription or omission.')],['AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Bleeding-Risk Assessment During AF Anticoagulation Decisions',
 'An older patient with AF and prior TIA has uncontrolled hypertension, frequent NSAID use and a high bleeding-risk score. There is no active bleeding or nonreversible contraindication to anticoagulation. Which approach is most appropriate?',
 'Correct modifiable bleeding risks and select appropriate anticoagulation with closer follow-up',[
 ('Withhold anticoagulation solely because the bleeding score is high','Bleeding scores identify hazards but overlap with stroke-risk factors and have limited discrimination. US guidance advises against using them alone to deny anticoagulation to a high-stroke-risk patient.'),
 ('Use an off-label reduced DOAC dose to compensate for the score','Dose reduction should follow validated drug-specific criteria, not a bleeding score alone. Unjustified underdosing may compromise embolic protection without a reliable safety benefit.'),
 ('Substitute aspirin to provide a safer equivalent stroke-prevention strategy','Aspirin does not provide equivalent AF stroke protection and can still cause significant bleeding, particularly with NSAIDs. It is not the standard substitute for indicated anticoagulation here.')],
 [S('Use the Score to Find Treatable Hazards','**Bleeding assessment supports safer prescribing rather than an automatic anticoagulation veto.** Age, hypertension and prior stroke can contribute to both stroke and bleeding risk, so a high score does not independently establish net harm.',
    'Control blood pressure, review NSAIDs and unnecessary antiplatelets, limit alcohol and investigate anemia or previous bleeding sources. Review renal and liver function, interactions and adherence.',
    'A documented active major bleed or another substantial contraindication requires a separate immediate assessment. The absence of such a condition in this stem matters to the decision.'),
  S('Prescribe the Right Treatment and Surveillance','**Choose a supported anticoagulant dose using the relevant label and clinical context.** A low dose is not inherently the correct dose merely because the patient is older or has a high bleeding score.',
    'For VKA treatment, optimize INR monitoring and time in therapeutic range. For DOACs, reassess renal function, weight, interactions and adherence at clinically appropriate intervals, especially after acute illness.',
    'Discuss warning symptoms, concomitant medicines and follow-up. A treatable source of bleeding does not necessarily create a permanent prohibition, while a nonreversible contraindication may warrant evaluation of alternatives such as selected left atrial appendage occlusion. Avoid promising that risk-factor correction eliminates all bleeding. The aim is a transparent net-benefit decision that preserves substantial stroke protection while reducing preventable harm, with reassessment when the patient’s condition changes.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

R['INVICTUS']=('INVICTUS randomized trial, 2022','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2209051')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Anticoagulation in Rheumatic Mitral Stenosis With AF',
 'A patient with AF has echocardiographically confirmed moderate-to-severe rheumatic mitral stenosis and no active bleeding. Which long-term anticoagulant is preferred?',
 'A vitamin K antagonist with appropriate INR monitoring',[
 ('Rivaroxaban at its standard AF dose','Rivaroxaban is useful for eligible AF without the relevant exclusion. INVICTUS found worse outcomes than VKA therapy in rheumatic-heart-disease-associated AF, supporting VKA treatment in this substrate.'),
 ('Apixaban at its standard AF dose','Apixaban is preferred to warfarin for many eligible AF patients, but significant mitral stenosis was excluded from pivotal DOAC evidence. It should not be assumed equivalent to VKA for this guideline-defined setting.'),
 ('Dabigatran at its standard AF dose','Direct thrombin inhibition is an option for selected non-stenotic AF populations. It does not replace the established VKA strategy in moderate-to-severe rheumatic mitral stenosis and is also unsuitable for mechanical valves.')],
 [S('Valve Substrate Changes the Anticoagulant Choice','**AF with moderate-or-greater mitral stenosis, particularly rheumatic disease, calls for VKA rather than a DOAC under US guidance.** The indication is not dependent on reaching the usual CHA2DS2-VASc threshold.',
    'For rheumatic mitral stenosis with AF, the usual warfarin INR range is ==2.0–3.0==. Mechanical prosthetic valves also require VKA, but their INR target depends on valve type, position and thromboembolic risk rather than one universal AF target.',
    'Do not classify every valve lesion as a DOAC exclusion. Mitral regurgitation and aortic valve disease differ from significant mitral stenosis, and bioprosthetic-valve decisions require their own timing and indication assessment.'),
  S('Interpret INVICTUS Without Overgeneralizing','**INVICTUS compared rivaroxaban with VKA therapy in rheumatic-heart-disease-associated AF.** VKA reduced the composite cardiovascular-event/death outcome without a significant increase in major bleeding.',
    'The result directly supports retaining VKA in this high-risk rheumatic substrate. It is not evidence that warfarin is superior to all DOACs in every form of AF or all valve disease.',
    'Provide INR access, interaction review and adherence support rather than selecting an unsupported agent solely to avoid monitoring. Assess valve severity, symptoms and suitability for valve intervention concurrently; anticoagulation prevents embolic events but does not relieve the fixed obstruction. Rhythm restoration or a later regular pulse does not remove the substrate-specific prevention issue. Review pregnancy plans and any other major prescribing constraints because they can alter the anticoagulation strategy and require specialist care.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024','INVICTUS'])

R['APIXLABEL']=('Apixaban US prescribing information, 2026','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2026/202155s042lbl.pdf')
R['RIVALABEL']=('Rivaroxaban US prescribing information','https://www.dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=10db92f9-2300-4a80-836b-673e1ae91610&type=display')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Apixaban Dose Reduction for AF',
 'An 82-year-old with AF weighs 58 kg and has stable serum creatinine 1.2 mg/dL. There is no severe hepatic disease, advanced renal failure or relevant interacting medication. Under US apixaban labelling, which AF dose is appropriate?',
 '2.5 mg twice daily',[
 ('5 mg twice daily','This is the usual AF dose when the reduction criteria are not met. The patient meets two criteria, age at least eighty and weight no more than sixty kilograms, despite creatinine below 1.5 mg/dL.'),
 ('2.5 mg once daily','The reduced AF regimen retains twice-daily administration. Halving the frequency is not the labelled reduction and can leave inadequate anticoagulant exposure.'),
 ('5 mg once daily','This is not a labelled AF regimen. Combining the daily amount of a twice-daily regimen into a single dose does not reproduce its exposure profile or evidence base.')],
 [S('Apply the Two-of-Three Rule','**US apixaban labelling reduces the AF dose to ==2.5 mg twice daily== when at least two specified characteristics are present.** They are age ==≥80 years==, weight ==≤60 kg== and serum creatinine ==≥1.5 mg/dL==.',
    'This patient qualifies through age and weight; all three are not required. Conversely, age alone does not automatically establish the reduced dose in an otherwise eligible patient.',
    'The usual AF dose is 5 mg twice daily when the applicable reduction rules are not met. Check the indication because acute VTE treatment and extended VTE prevention use different regimens.'),
  S('Dose Selection Requires More Than This Rule','**Review renal and hepatic status and drug interactions before prescribing.** The stem excludes additional conditions that could change the decision or require specialist assessment.',
    'Strong combined P-gp/CYP3A4 inhibitors can require modification or avoidance, while strong inducers can reduce exposure. Local labels differ in some severe-renal-disease circumstances, so do not turn a US-label question into an unqualified worldwide rule.',
    'Use the actual current weight and reliable laboratory data, and reassess after substantial weight change or acute illness. Do not reduce a dose solely from fear of bleeding when validated criteria are absent, or retain the full dose when criteria are present. Explain adherence and missed-dose instructions using the specific product information. A routine INR does not titrate apixaban in the way it titrates warfarin. The correct strength and frequency are both essential; matching a nominal daily milligram total does not establish an evidence-based regimen.')],['APIXLABEL','AFUS2023'],
 table=(['US AF reduction criterion','Threshold'],[['Age','≥80 years'],['Body weight','≤60 kg'],['Serum creatinine','≥1.5 mg/dL'],['Requirement','At least 2 of 3 → 2.5 mg twice daily']]))

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Strong Enzyme Induction and DOAC Exposure',
 'A patient with AF takes apixaban and requires rifampin for a newly diagnosed infection. Kidney function is stable. Which anticoagulation approach best addresses the interaction?',
 'Avoid the apixaban–rifampin combination and coordinate an alternative anticoagulation plan',[
 ('Increase the apixaban dose empirically to compensate for induction','There is no validated higher apixaban regimen that reliably compensates for rifampin induction. Exposure changes over time, and empirical escalation can cause unsafe dosing when rifampin is later withdrawn; the label recommends avoiding the combination.'),
 ('Reduce apixaban because rifampin increases its concentration','Rifampin is a strong inducer that lowers apixaban exposure, the opposite of a strong inhibitor. Reducing the dose would further threaten anticoagulant effectiveness.'),
 ('Separate administration by twelve hours while retaining the same regimen','Enzyme and transporter induction persists beyond the moment the drugs are swallowed. Dose separation does not remove the interaction and is not the labelled management strategy.')],
 [S('Inducers and Inhibitors Have Opposite Consequences','**Rifampin induces P-gp and CYP3A4 and can reduce apixaban exposure, increasing thromboembolic risk.** US prescribing information advises avoiding this combination.',
    'Other important strong inducers include carbamazepine, phenytoin and St John’s wort. Review prescriptions, supplements and new treatments rather than assessing only the anticoagulant list.',
    'Strong combined inhibitors can instead increase exposure and bleeding. Their management is drug- and dose-specific; a generic instruction to halve every DOAC with every interacting drug is unsafe.'),
  S('Reassess the Whole Anticoagulation Regimen','**Coordinate infection treatment and anticoagulation so that indicated stroke protection is maintained.** An alternative agent or strategy may be needed; simply stopping apixaban without a plan can leave preventable risk.',
    'Warfarin is also affected by rifampin, but its response can be followed with INR and careful adjustment when selected. Induction and its offset require follow-up during initiation and discontinuation.',
    'Assess renal function with the method required by the chosen drug’s evidence and label. For example, US rivaroxaban AF dosing uses creatinine clearance with 20 mg daily above 50 mL/min and 15 mg daily at or below 50 mL/min within the applicable renal-use provisions, taken with the evening meal; severe renal disease requires additional restrictions and evidence review. Do not transfer one drug’s age-weight-creatinine rule to another. Revisit dosing after AKI, renal recovery or interacting-drug withdrawal, and do not use routine INR as a universal DOAC concentration assay.')],['APIXLABEL','RIVALABEL','AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Antiplatelets Are Not Equivalent AF Stroke Prevention',
 'A patient with clinical AF, prior TIA and hypertension is eligible for oral anticoagulation and has no coronary antiplatelet indication. The patient asks whether aspirin can provide comparable protection with less treatment burden. Which response is best?',
 'Recommend appropriate anticoagulation; aspirin is not an equivalent substitute for AF stroke prevention',[
 ('Use aspirin alone because it protects against arterial thrombotic events','Aspirin has established roles in selected atherosclerotic disease, but those benefits do not make it equivalent for AF-related embolism. The prior TIA strengthens rather than removes the anticoagulation indication.'),
 ('Use aspirin plus clopidogrel to reproduce anticoagulant protection','Dual antiplatelet therapy is useful in selected coronary settings, but it does not provide an equivalent benefit-risk substitute for anticoagulation in an eligible high-risk AF patient.'),
 ('Use clopidogrel alone as secondary prevention after the TIA','Clopidogrel monotherapy is appropriate for selected noncardioembolic TIA or ischemic stroke. Established AF supplies a cardioembolic prevention indication here, for which antiplatelet monotherapy does not provide equivalent protection to anticoagulation.')],
 [S('Match the Drug to the Thromboembolic Mechanism','**Oral anticoagulation is the established preventive treatment for eligible AF patients at sufficient stroke risk.** Aspirin, alone or with clopidogrel, is not recommended as an alternative when anticoagulation is indicated.',
    'Antiplatelets treat platelet-mediated atherothrombotic disease in selected contexts. AF-related left atrial thrombus and systemic embolism require a different evidence-based prevention strategy.',
    'Prior stroke or TIA contributes two points in the familiar risk schemes. A patient does not become low risk because AF is intermittent, rate-controlled or asymptomatic.'),
  S('Address Treatment Burden Directly','**Explore the actual concern about anticoagulation rather than offering a weaker substitute as equivalent.** Discuss administration, monitoring, cost, interactions and bleeding risk, and choose an eligible agent with an appropriate dose.',
    'DOACs avoid routine INR titration but still require adherence and clinical review. VKA remains necessary in particular valve substrates and can be supported with organized monitoring.',
    'Aspirin also causes bleeding, so it cannot be described as harmless protection. If a genuine contraindication to long-term anticoagulation exists, evaluate that condition and selected alternatives rather than automatically prescribing dual antiplatelets. Continue risk-factor treatment and symptom management alongside prevention. Review any existing antiplatelet prescription for a separate coronary or vascular indication; adding it without an indication increases bleeding burden. Document the agreed plan and revisit it when comorbidity or patient priorities change.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

R['AFIRE']=('AFIRE randomized trial, 2019','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1904143')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'AF With Stable Coronary Disease Beyond One Year',
 'A patient requires long-term anticoagulation for AF and underwent PCI three years ago. There has been no recurrent ACS, stent thrombosis or other antiplatelet indication. Which chronic antithrombotic approach best follows US 2023 guidance?',
 'Oral anticoagulant monotherapy',[
 ('Oral anticoagulant plus lifelong aspirin','Aspirin has an important early role in selected coronary pathways, but routine addition beyond a year in this stable AF setting increases bleeding without a sufficient established benefit to justify continued combination therapy.'),
 ('Oral anticoagulant plus lifelong clopidogrel','A P2Y12 inhibitor is commonly combined with anticoagulation during a defined period after PCI. The remote uncomplicated procedure and absence of stent thrombosis support withdrawing unnecessary chronic antiplatelet exposure.'),
 ('Aspirin plus clopidogrel without anticoagulation','Dual antiplatelet therapy can protect a recent coronary stent in appropriate circumstances. It does not replace the separate long-term anticoagulation requirement for AF stroke prevention.')],
 [S('Separate Remote Coronary Disease From Recent PCI','**US 2023 recommends OAC alone in AF with chronic coronary disease beyond ==1 year after revascularization==, or CAD not requiring revascularization, when there is no history of stent thrombosis.** The purpose is to reduce major bleeding from unnecessary combination treatment.',
    'Recent ACS, recent PCI, prior stent thrombosis and other selected high-ischemic-risk circumstances require their own antithrombotic plan. This recommendation should not be used to omit essential early post-PCI treatment.',
    'Review the actual indication and intended stop date of each antiplatelet, rather than perpetuating aspirin because it was started years earlier.'),
  S('Interpret AFIRE Within Its Population','**AFIRE supports anticoagulant monotherapy in AF with stable coronary disease.** Rivaroxaban alone was noninferior for the composite efficacy outcome and safer for major bleeding than rivaroxaban plus a single antiplatelet.',
    'The study was conducted in Japan using its approved rivaroxaban dose framework. Its trial doses should not be copied as universal AF doses in other jurisdictions.',
    'This is different from low-dose rivaroxaban plus aspirin for selected vascular disease without an AF-dose anticoagulation requirement. Continue appropriate coronary secondary prevention, including lipid, pressure and lifestyle treatment; removing an unnecessary antiplatelet does not mean abandoning coronary care. Confirm renal dosing, adherence and any new ischemic event at follow-up. A new ACS or intervention can change the plan, so monotherapy is the best current strategy for the supplied stable history rather than a permanent rule regardless of future events.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024','AFIRE'])

R['BRIDGE']=('BRIDGE randomized trial, 2015','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1501035')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Temporary Warfarin Interruption Without Routine Bridging',
 'A patient taking warfarin for AF requires an elective procedure for which interruption is necessary. There is no mechanical valve, recent stroke or TIA, or other exceptional thromboembolic indication. Which perioperative strategy is generally appropriate?',
 'Temporarily interrupt warfarin without therapeutic heparin bridging and restart when procedural hemostasis permits',[
 ('Bridge with therapeutic LMWH whenever the INR falls below two','A subtherapeutic INR during planned interruption is expected and does not itself establish a bridging indication. Routine therapeutic bridging adds bleeding without established benefit in the usual AF population described.'),
 ('Bridge with IV unfractionated heparin instead of LMWH to avoid the excess bleeding problem','A shorter-acting agent can be appropriate in selected exceptional circumstances, but changing the heparin formulation does not create a need for routine bridging when the AF indication does not warrant it.'),
 ('Replace warfarin with aspirin during the interruption','Aspirin is not a validated substitute for AF anticoagulation during interruption and may add perioperative bleeding. The interruption and restart plan should follow procedural and thromboembolic assessment.')],
 [S('First Decide Whether Interruption Is Needed','**Some procedures can be performed with continuing anticoagulation, while others require a planned interruption.** The stem establishes that interruption is necessary, so the question concerns bridging rather than a universal rule to stop warfarin.',
    'US 2023 recommends temporary cessation without bridging for most AF patients, excluding important exceptional groups such as mechanical valves and recent stroke or TIA from this general statement.',
    'Assess procedural bleeding consequences, renal function, concomitant antiplatelets and the complete thromboembolic history. A generic score or an expected low INR should not replace that assessment.'),
  S('Interpret BRIDGE and Plan Resumption','**BRIDGE found that foregoing LMWH bridging was noninferior for arterial thromboembolism and reduced major bleeding in the studied warfarin-treated AF population.** Major bleeding was 1.3% without bridging versus 3.2% with bridging.',
    'Mechanical valves and recent embolic events were excluded, and the highest-risk AF groups were underrepresented. Do not extrapolate the result as proof that no patient ever needs individualized perioperative protection.',
    'A common elective warfarin interruption is about five days, with INR assessment and restart once adequate hemostasis and oral administration are possible. Exact timing depends on the procedure and clinical course. DOAC interruption instead depends on agent, clearance and bleeding risk, and routine heparin bridging is generally unnecessary. Distinguish therapeutic bridging from separately indicated postoperative venous thromboprophylaxis. Document the restart plan so a short intended pause does not become an unintended prolonged withdrawal of stroke protection.')],['AFUS2023','BRIDGE'])

R['CHAMPION2026']=('CHAMPION-AF randomized trial, 2026','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2517213')
R['CLOSURE2026']=('CLOSURE-AF randomized trial, 2026','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2513310')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Percutaneous Left Atrial Appendage Occlusion',
 'A patient has AF without significant mitral stenosis or a mechanical valve, high thromboembolic risk and recurrent severe bleeding from a nonreversible cause that precludes long-term oral anticoagulation. Multidisciplinary assessment finds suitable anatomy and an acceptable procedural and short-term antithrombotic plan. Which strategy is reasonable to evaluate?',
 'Percutaneous left atrial appendage occlusion',[
 ('Aspirin plus clopidogrel as an equivalent indefinite substitute','Dual antiplatelet therapy is not equivalent to anticoagulation for AF stroke prevention. A device-associated short-term antithrombotic regimen is a separate question from relying indefinitely on DAPT alone.'),
 ('Empirical low-dose anticoagulation below the approved regimen','Unvalidated dose reduction does not reliably resolve a nonreversible bleeding contraindication and can leave inadequate embolic protection. Dose selection must follow evidence rather than an assumed compromise.'),
 ('AF ablation with withdrawal of stroke prevention after sinus rhythm returns','Ablation treats the rhythm substrate and symptoms but does not guarantee elimination of AF or stroke risk. Rhythm success is not an established substitute for prevention in this high-risk patient.')],
 [S('Select the Appropriate Contraindication Setting','**US 2023 considers percutaneous LAAO reasonable in AF with moderate-to-high stroke risk and a nonreversible contraindication to long-term OAC.** It is not the routine first choice for every patient who dislikes taking tablets.',
    'Differentiate nonreversible bleeding risk from a treatable lesion or isolated procedural bleeding. When a source can be corrected, resuming appropriate anticoagulation may remain the best-supported strategy.',
    'The evidence for OAC is more extensive, and device treatment has its own procedural and follow-up burdens. Shared decision-making should address both rather than portraying occlusion as risk-free anticoagulation avoidance.'),
  S('Plan the Procedure and Subsequent Protection','**Assess appendage anatomy and exclude thrombus before implantation using the appropriate imaging protocol.** Consider vascular complications, pericardial effusion or tamponade, embolization, residual leak and device-related thrombus.',
    'Postimplant antithrombotic requirements depend on the device, bleeding profile and protocol. Inability to take indefinite OAC does not mean that no short-term antithrombotic treatment or surveillance will be needed.',
    'Arrange follow-up imaging for residual leak or device thrombus. New evidence must be interpreted by population: CHAMPION-AF (2026), in anticoagulation-eligible patients, met noninferiority for cardiovascular death/stroke/systemic embolism and reduced nonprocedure-related bleeding. CLOSURE-AF (2026), in patients at high stroke and bleeding risk, did not establish noninferiority to physician-directed medical care for its different composite. These results do not establish universal device superiority or remove procedural risk. Surgical appendage exclusion is a distinct intervention and does not automatically permit OAC withdrawal. Continue vascular prevention and reassess other anticoagulation indications.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024','CHAMPION2026','CLOSURE2026'])

R['ARTESIA']=('ARTESiA randomized trial, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2310234')
R['NOAH']=('NOAH-AFNET 6 randomized trial, 2023','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2303062')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Anticoagulation Evidence in Device-Detected Subclinical AF',
 'A patient without prior clinical AF has confirmed device-detected atrial episodes lasting 10–40 minutes and elevated stroke-risk factors. Which statement best summarizes ARTESiA and NOAH-AFNET 6 for counseling?',
 'Anticoagulation requires individualized benefit–bleeding assessment; the trials do not support treating every brief device episode identically',[
 ('A six-minute episode establishes the same treatment indication as clinical AF at the same risk score','The six-minute entry criterion is not a universal treatment threshold. ARTESiA showed a stroke/systemic-embolism reduction with more major bleeding, while NOAH did not significantly reduce its broader primary efficacy composite.'),
 ('ARTESiA found similar stroke prevention with aspirin and apixaban, favoring aspirin on bleeding grounds','ARTESiA found fewer stroke/systemic-embolism events with apixaban but more major bleeding. Its efficacy result cannot be summarized as equivalence; the decision requires considering both absolute benefit and harm.'),
 ('NOAH demonstrated a significant reduction in its primary efficacy composite, offset by more major bleeding','NOAH did not demonstrate a significant reduction in cardiovascular death, stroke or systemic embolism and increased its death-or-major-bleeding safety composite. An offsetting statistically significant efficacy benefit is not what the trial established.')],
 [S('Verify the Rhythm Before Applying Trial Evidence','**Device-detected atrial high-rate episodes require review of stored signals because noise, oversensing and other atrial rhythms can produce alerts.** Confirm duration and burden rather than treating the device label alone as a diagnosis.',
    'Subclinical device-detected AF differs from established clinical AF populations in which anticoagulation benefit is well supported. A similar risk score does not guarantee an identical untreated event rate.',
    'Assess bleeding, comorbidity, episode progression and patient preferences. ESC 2024 permits considering DOAC treatment in selected high-stroke-risk patients without major bleeding risk, while acknowledging uncertainty about an optimal episode-duration threshold.'),
  S('Compare Different Endpoints and Populations','**ARTESiA studied subclinical AF lasting six minutes to 24 hours.** Apixaban reduced stroke/systemic embolism versus aspirin but increased major bleeding; an efficacy result cannot be discussed without its bleeding consequence.',
    'NOAH-AFNET 6 studied atrial high-rate episodes without established ECG-diagnosed AF. Edoxaban did not significantly reduce cardiovascular death, stroke or systemic embolism and increased the composite of death or major bleeding; it was stopped early.',
    'The studies used different drugs, comparators and primary endpoints, so they are not a head-to-head comparison or a simple contradiction. Discuss absolute risk and surveillance, not just relative treatment effects. Continue follow-up for longer episodes or clinical AF, which can change the decision. Aspirin as a trial comparator does not establish it as a generally effective substitute for indicated anticoagulation in clinical AF.')],['ARTESIA','NOAH','AF2024'],
 table=(['Trial','Comparison','Main interpretation'],[
 ['ARTESiA','Apixaban versus aspirin','Stroke/systemic embolism 0.78% versus 1.24%/patient-year; on-treatment major bleeding 1.71% versus 0.94%/patient-year'],
 ['NOAH-AFNET 6','Edoxaban versus no anticoagulation/placebo-based control','No significant primary efficacy-composite reduction; more death/major-bleeding composite']]))

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Dynamic Stroke-Risk Reassessment in AF',
 'A man with documented paroxysmal AF was initially 60 years old with no stroke-risk factors and did not receive anticoagulation. At review he is 67 and has developed hypertension and diabetes. He reports few palpitations and his office ECG is sinus rhythm. Which approach is appropriate?',
 'Recalculate thromboembolic risk and initiate appropriate anticoagulation if no contraindication is found',[
 ('Continue the original no-anticoagulation plan because it was correct at diagnosis','The original decision may have been appropriate, but age and new comorbidities change current risk. A baseline assessment is not a permanent exemption from prevention.'),
 ('Require AF on the current office ECG before changing prevention','A sinus snapshot does not remove a verified paroxysmal AF diagnosis. Current stroke-risk factors should be assessed even when the rhythm is not captured during the visit.'),
 ('Use palpitation frequency as the principal measure of embolic risk','Symptoms correlate imperfectly with AF burden and do not replace established clinical risk factors. Infrequent palpitations cannot neutralize the new age, hypertension and diabetes contributions.')],
 [S('Risk Evolves While Symptoms May Improve','**AF prevention requires periodic reassessment because age and incident disease can change net benefit.** This patient now has age 65–74, hypertension and diabetes, giving three non-sex risk points.',
    'Both ESC 2024 and US 2023 frameworks support anticoagulation at this risk level in an otherwise eligible clinical AF patient. The office rhythm and perceived symptom burden do not negate the indication.',
    'Review the accuracy of new diagnoses and the complete history, including prior embolism, vascular disease and HF. Avoid copying a prior score forward without updating its components.'),
  S('Reassess Safety and the Whole Care Plan','**Update renal function, weight, interactions and bleeding risks before choosing the drug and dose.** Prevention changes should be accompanied by adherence support and a review plan.',
    'Risk reassessment continues after spontaneous sinus rhythm or successful rhythm treatment; rhythm status alone is not a universal stop rule. Postablation decisions require the applicable guideline, patient profile and current evidence rather than extrapolation from a single normal ECG.',
    'Continue pressure, diabetes, weight and other comorbidity management and revisit rate or rhythm treatment if symptoms or ventricular function change. Explain that a new recommendation does not mean the earlier low-risk decision was necessarily wrong: the clinical facts have changed. Conversely, do not delay a warranted update until the next symptomatic attack. Document both the revised risk estimate and the selected treatment so future follow-up can assess benefit, adverse effects and any further change in circumstances.')],['AF2024','AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Rate Control in Stable AF With Preserved LV Function',
 'A stable adult has AF with a ventricular rate of 145/min, BP 136/82 mmHg and LVEF 60%. There is no pre-excitation, bronchospasm or acute HF. Rate control is selected. Which initial drug class is guideline-supported?',
 'A beta blocker or diltiazem/verapamil chosen according to comorbidity and response',[
 ('Digoxin as the preferred initial monotherapy','Digoxin has useful roles when other agents are unsuitable and in selected HF patients. Its slower onset and limited control during high sympathetic drive make it less suitable than the standard initial agents in this uncomplicated stable setting.'),
 ('Amiodarone as the preferred initial monotherapy','Amiodarone can help selected critically ill or decompensated patients when standard agents are unsuitable. Its adverse effects and potential rhythm conversion make it disproportionate as routine first-line rate control here.'),
 ('Flecainide as an AV nodal rate-control agent','Flecainide is a rhythm-control drug for selected substrates, not a standard AV nodal rate-control treatment. It can organize atrial arrhythmia with rapid conduction if used without appropriate safeguards.')],
 [S('Use EF and Perfusion to Select the Agent','**US 2023 supports beta blockers or nondihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers for stable rapid AF, with diltiazem/verapamil requiring ==EF >40%==.** Pressure, symptoms and comorbidity determine the individual choice.',
    'An acceptable EF alone does not prove that any bolus is safe. Assess acute congestion, low output, medication exposure and conduction disease before treatment.',
    'Pre-excited AF is a separate emergency in which these AV nodal strategies are contraindicated. Confirm the ECG phenotype before applying an ordinary AF rate-control pathway.'),
  S('Titrate to Clinical Benefit','**Follow rate, blood pressure, perfusion and symptoms rather than pursuing an arbitrary pulse at the expense of circulation.** Treat infection, hypovolemia, electrolyte disturbance and other triggers concurrently.',
    'Avoid stacking IV beta blockade and diltiazem/verapamil without careful supervision because their nodal and myocardial effects can combine. Digoxin is an option when standard agents are ineffective or contraindicated, with renal and interaction assessment.',
    'IV amiodarone may be considered in selected critical illness or decompensated HF when alternatives are unsuitable, but possible cardioversion raises thromboembolic considerations. Acute instability attributable to AF calls for synchronized cardioversion. Reassess EF if tachycardia persists or HF develops, because the substrate and preferred treatment can change. Rate control does not replace stroke prevention, and satisfactory resting control may still leave exercise symptoms requiring further assessment.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

R['DIGLABEL']=('Digoxin prescribing information','https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=1b7e5c6e-e052-41b0-a4ce-05dfe62faaaf')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Digoxin Interaction With Amiodarone',
 'An older patient with AF takes a stable digoxin regimen. Amiodarone is added for a separate rhythm-control indication. Kidney function is moderately reduced but unchanged. Which adjustment best addresses the important interaction?',
 'Reduce digoxin exposure through dose or frequency adjustment and monitor concentrations and clinical response',[
 ('Keep the dose unchanged because the interaction depends on new AKI','Amiodarone can increase digoxin exposure without a new creatinine rise. Stable renal function does not remove the transporter-mediated interaction or the patient’s baseline vulnerability.'),
 ('Increase digoxin because amiodarone lowers its concentration','Amiodarone generally increases digoxin concentration rather than lowering it. Increasing the dose can worsen bradyarrhythmia and systemic toxicity.'),
 ('Separate digoxin and amiodarone administration by twelve hours without changing the regimen','Dose separation can help some absorption interactions, but it does not prevent the persistent pharmacokinetic effect of amiodarone on digoxin exposure. Dose or frequency adjustment and clinical and concentration monitoring remain necessary.')],
 [S('Recognize a Narrow-Therapeutic-Index Interaction','**Amiodarone can increase digoxin concentrations through pharmacokinetic effects including P-gp inhibition.** Age, low lean body mass and renal impairment can further increase toxicity risk.',
    'The digoxin label advises measuring concentrations before adding interacting drugs and reducing the dose by approximately ==30–50%== or modifying the dosing frequency with continued monitoring for this interaction.',
    'Individualize the adjustment rather than treating the percentage as a guarantee. Review the continuing indication for both drugs and other agents that slow sinus or AV nodal function.'),
  S('Interpret Concentrations With Clinical Findings','**US 2023 considers a serum digoxin target ==below 1.2 ng/mL== reasonable when levels are indicated for AF care.** A reported concentration is not an independent diagnosis of safety or toxicity.',
    'Obtain a postdistribution sample, generally at least six hours after the dose, and interpret it with dose timing and renal function. An early sample can overstate the clinically relevant circulating concentration.',
    'Assess nausea, anorexia, confusion, visual symptoms, bradycardia, AV block and ventricular arrhythmias. Check potassium and magnesium because depletion increases susceptibility even at a seemingly acceptable level. Digoxin can help rate control in selected HF or drug-limited patients but is less effective during high sympathetic drive. Serious suspected toxicity requires urgent assessment and consideration of digoxin immune Fab according to severity. Reassess during AKI or recovery and after starting or stopping interacting therapy; a previously stable prescription is not permanently safe.')],['DIGLABEL','AFUS2023'])

R['RACEII']=('RACE II randomized trial, 2010','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1001337')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Lenient Rate Control in an Appropriate AF Patient',
 'A patient with permanent AF has preserved LVEF, no HF symptoms, satisfactory daily activity and a resting ventricular rate of 96/min on tolerated treatment. Which interpretation best fits a lenient initial rate-control strategy?',
 'The resting rate can be acceptable, with continued symptom and functional reassessment',[
 ('Treatment must be intensified until the resting rate is below eighty in every patient','A stricter target can help selected symptoms or ventricular dysfunction, but RACE II did not establish a universal requirement for strict resting control in suitable stable permanent AF.'),
 ('The rate proves that exercise control is adequate without further clinical assessment','A resting number does not establish the response during activity. Persistent exertional symptoms require assessment even when the resting target is met.'),
 ('Rate treatment can be stopped because the pulse is below one hundred and ten','The observed rate may reflect effective medication. Meeting a target is not evidence that treatment is unnecessary, and withdrawal can allow recurrence of rapid conduction.')],
 [S('Apply the Target to the Right Clinical Setting','**A resting rate below ==110/min== is a reasonable lenient initial target in selected patients, especially when symptoms and ventricular function are satisfactory.** A target is a clinical strategy rather than a declaration that every rate below it is harmless.',
    'RACE II compared lenient resting control below 110/min with strict control below 80/min at rest and below 110/min during moderate exercise in permanent AF.',
    'Lenient control was noninferior for the trial’s composite outcome and easier to achieve. The study does not prove equivalence in every AF population or justify ignoring persistent symptoms.'),
  S('Tighten or Change the Strategy When Needed','**Symptoms, ventricular dysfunction and suspected arrhythmia-induced cardiomyopathy can justify a stricter target or rhythm-control assessment.** Assess exercise behavior and the overall rhythm burden rather than relying on a single clinic pulse.',
    'Avoid excessive nodal suppression that causes dizziness, hypotension, pauses or functional limitation. A numerically lower rate is not necessarily a better outcome when adverse effects outweigh benefit.',
    'Review comorbidities and reversible drivers, medication adherence and ventricular function as indicated. Continue stroke prevention independently of satisfactory rate control. In patients with CRT, adequate biventricular capture creates another important rate-management objective that cannot be reduced to the usual resting boundary. Explain that lenient management includes surveillance and reassessment; it is not discharge from care or permission for prolonged rapid rates during ordinary activity. The supplied patient is clinically doing well, so automatic dose escalation offers no established advantage solely to reach a stricter number.')],['RACEII','AF2024','AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Emergency Cardioversion for Hemodynamically Unstable AF',
 'A patient with AF of uncertain duration develops a ventricular rate of 180/min followed by severe hypotension, pulmonary edema and confusion attributable to the rhythm. A pulse is present. Which immediate approach is appropriate?',
 'Perform synchronized electrical cardioversion without delaying for elective thrombus-exclusion or anticoagulation prerequisites',[
 ('Wait for three weeks of therapeutic anticoagulation before cardioversion','A preparatory anticoagulation period is relevant to elective cardioversion. Persistent life-threatening instability requires immediate treatment; an elective delay can worsen shock and pulmonary edema.'),
 ('Obtain transesophageal echo before allowing cardioversion','TEE can facilitate selected early elective conversion, but it must not postpone treatment of an unstable patient. Thromboembolic management should be addressed promptly alongside resuscitation rather than used as a barrier to it.'),
 ('Complete sequential IV rate-control drug trials before cardioversion','Rate drugs are appropriate in many stable cases, but prolonged trials can worsen pressure or delay effective rhythm treatment in attributable shock. Immediate electrical conversion is the preferred emergency strategy here.')],
 [S('Instability Overrides Elective Preparation','**Immediate electrical cardioversion is indicated for hemodynamic instability caused by AF with rapid ventricular response.** Confirm that AF is contributing materially rather than merely accompanying another cause of shock.',
    'Support airway, ventilation and circulation, apply monitoring and treat relevant triggers concurrently. A pulse identifies a cardioversion-with-pulse pathway; pulseless deterioration requires immediate reassessment and the appropriate arrest treatment.',
    'Uncertain duration raises embolic concern but does not justify a life-threatening delay. Start appropriate anticoagulation as soon as feasible when not contraindicated and make a postconversion prevention plan.'),
  S('Use Current Synchronized Technique','**AHA 2025 considers an initial biphasic setting of ==at least 200 J== reasonable for adult AF cardioversion, with escalation after failure according to the device.** This differs from older low-energy escalation teaching.',
    'Verify synchronization markers on ventricular depolarization and recheck after each shock. Provide sedation when feasible without delaying essential treatment in a rapidly deteriorating patient.',
    'Assess the resulting rhythm and circulation, not just the defibrillator’s success message. Correct pad contact and positioning, reconsider triggers and obtain expert assistance for refractory episodes. Evaluate stroke risk, duration and anticoagulation after stabilization because restored sinus rhythm does not end embolic risk. Distinguish synchronized treatment of AF with a pulse from unsynchronized defibrillation for VF or polymorphic VT; irregularity alone does not make an AF shock automatically unsynchronized.')],['ALS2025','AFUS2023','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'ESC 2024 Preparation for Elective Cardioversion',
 'A stable patient has a reliably documented AF episode lasting 36 hours and has not received anticoagulation. Elective cardioversion is being considered. Under ESC 2024, which preparation is appropriate?',
 'Use at least three weeks of therapeutic anticoagulation or a thrombus-exclusion imaging strategy before conversion',[
 ('Proceed without thrombus precautions because the episode is under forty-eight hours','This imports an older simplified duration rule into a specifically named ESC 2024 question. ESC uses a safety-first approach beyond twenty-four hours, and shorter duration does not itself eliminate embolic risk.'),
 ('Give one anticoagulant dose and proceed without imaging because the duration is known','A single dose does not establish the recommended pretreatment interval or exclude existing thrombus. Early conversion can be considered through an appropriate imaging-guided pathway with anticoagulation.'),
 ('Use aspirin for three weeks before conversion instead of therapeutic anticoagulation','Aspirin is not the required anticoagulant preparation and does not provide equivalent thrombus protection. The duration of an ineffective substitute does not make the strategy adequate.')],
 [S('Name the Guideline When Duration Rules Differ','**ESC 2024 advises against elective cardioversion after AF lasting more than ==24 hours== unless at least ==3 weeks== of therapeutic anticoagulation has been given or intracardiac thrombus is excluded by TOE.** The supplied 36-hour episode falls within that precaution.',
    'US 2023 states the three-week-or-imaging recommendation for duration at least 48 hours, with additional risk-based consideration below that duration. These frameworks should not be silently interchanged in an exam question.',
    'Uncertain onset is not reliably short onset. Palpitation onset can underestimate AF duration because preceding episodes may be asymptomatic.'),
  S('Ensure the Preparation Is Actually Therapeutic','**Confirm uninterrupted appropriate anticoagulation, including DOAC adherence or therapeutic VKA control.** A prescription date alone does not prove three weeks of effective treatment.',
    'If earlier elective conversion is clinically useful, use an appropriate imaging-guided protocol with anticoagulation rather than treating imaging as a substitute for all peri-procedural protection.',
    'Continue appropriate anticoagulation after conversion because atrial mechanical dysfunction and early recurrence can sustain risk. Long-term continuation depends on stroke risk rather than a single successful shock. Immediate life-threatening instability follows the emergency pathway and must not be delayed for elective prerequisites. Discuss treatment alternatives, sedation and procedural risks while maintaining rate and symptom control during preparation. The main distinction is between a stable elective opportunity and an emergency requiring immediate rescue.')],['AF2024','AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'TEE-Guided Early Cardioversion',
 'A stable symptomatic patient has AF lasting two weeks and has not completed three weeks of anticoagulation. Earlier cardioversion is desired. Which strategy appropriately permits consideration of earlier conversion?',
 'Establish therapeutic anticoagulation and use TEE to exclude atrial thrombus, with continued anticoagulation afterward',[
 ('Use a normal transthoracic echo to exclude left atrial appendage thrombus','TTE provides essential structural information but does not reliably exclude appendage thrombus. A normal EF or absence of a visible mass on TTE is not an adequate substitute for the appropriate thrombus study.'),
 ('Perform TEE and omit anticoagulation if no thrombus is visible','A negative study reduces concern for existing thrombus but does not prevent new thrombus during postconversion atrial stunning or recurrent AF. Peri- and postconversion anticoagulation remain necessary in this setting.'),
 ('Use the absence of embolic symptoms as evidence that no atrial thrombus exists','Atrial thrombus can be clinically silent until embolization occurs. A symptom history cannot replace the recommended pretreatment or imaging pathway.')],
 [S('Imaging Answers Whether Thrombus Is Present Now','**TEE, also termed TOE, evaluates the left atrium and appendage for thrombus before an early cardioversion strategy.** It can shorten the wait in an appropriately anticoagulated and assessed patient.',
    'Assess image quality and distinguish definite thrombus from spontaneous echo contrast or other structures. An indeterminate study needs clarification rather than being relabelled negative.',
    'Delayed-contrast cardiac CT is an alternative in selected experienced pathways, but ordinary non-dedicated CT or a normal TTE should not be assumed equivalent.'),
  S('Maintain Protection Across Conversion','**A negative TEE does not remove postconversion thromboembolic risk.** Atrial mechanical recovery can lag behind electrical sinus restoration, and early AF recurrence may be silent.',
    'US guidance generally requires therapeutic anticoagulation before conversion and continuation for at least four weeks afterward. Longer treatment follows the patient’s stroke-risk indication rather than the imaging result alone.',
    'If thrombus is identified, defer elective cardioversion, treat with an appropriate anticoagulant regimen and repeat imaging after a suitable treatment interval. Confirm dose and adherence, particularly if thrombus appears despite prescribed therapy. Imaging-guided conversion does not make an unstable patient wait unnecessarily; emergency management remains a separate decision. Explain the test’s purpose and limitations so the patient understands why a reassuring image can coexist with a continuing need for anticoagulation.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Left Atrial Appendage Thrombus Before Elective Cardioversion',
 'TEE before planned elective cardioversion shows definite left atrial appendage thrombus. The patient is stable and has not previously received anticoagulation. Under US 2023 guidance, which next strategy is appropriate?',
 'Defer cardioversion, give therapeutic anticoagulation for three to six weeks and repeat imaging before reconsidering conversion',[
 ('Proceed with cardioversion after one therapeutic heparin dose','Immediate anticoagulant effect does not establish thrombus resolution. In a stable elective setting, conversion with known thrombus risks embolization and should be deferred.'),
 ('Use pharmacological conversion instead of an electrical shock','Embolic risk relates to rhythm conversion and atrial recovery, not merely the electrical energy. Drug-induced conversion does not make a known appendage thrombus safe.'),
 ('Anticoagulate for the stated interval and then cardiovert without repeat imaging','Elapsed treatment does not prove resolution. The guideline calls for repeat imaging before reconsidering cardioversion when a definite thrombus was previously demonstrated.')],
 [S('A Definite Thrombus Changes the Elective Plan','**Known atrial thrombus requires deferral of elective cardioversion and effective anticoagulant treatment.** The stable patient can undergo treatment and reassessment rather than taking an avoidable embolic risk.',
    'US 2023 specifies at least a ==3–6-week== therapeutic course before repeat imaging. ESC 2024 describes a minimum ==4 weeks== followed by repeat TOE; explicit attribution prevents treating different phrasing as one exact universal interval.',
    'Choose an anticoagulant appropriate to the valve substrate, renal and hepatic status, interactions and adherence. A mechanical valve or significant mitral stenosis requires the corresponding VKA strategy.'),
  S('Confirm Resolution Rather Than Assuming It','**Repeat TEE or an appropriate alternative imaging protocol determines whether the thrombus has resolved.** If it persists, reassess the diagnosis and anticoagulant exposure before deciding the next step.',
    'When thrombus occurs despite prescribed treatment, review missed doses, dose criteria, interacting drugs, absorption and VKA control. An alternative anticoagulant can be considered in selected cases, but evidence does not establish one universally superior switch.',
    'Do not replace resolution assessment with a normal pulse, absence of embolic symptoms or completion of an arbitrary calendar period. Pharmacological and electrical conversion share thromboembolic precautions. Continue indicated prevention after eventual successful conversion because atrial stunning and recurrence remain relevant. A later life-threatening deterioration requires urgent specialist resuscitation decisions, but that exception should not be imported into this stable elective scenario to justify premature conversion.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Anticoagulation After Successful AF Cardioversion',
 'A 58-year-old with no chronic stroke-risk factors has AF lasting five days. TEE excludes thrombus, therapeutic anticoagulation is established and cardioversion restores sinus rhythm. There is no bleeding contraindication. Under US 2023 guidance, what is the appropriate immediate postconversion anticoagulation plan?',
 'Continue therapeutic anticoagulation for at least four weeks, then reassess the long-term indication',[
 ('Stop anticoagulation immediately because TEE excluded thrombus','The scan assesses pre-existing thrombus, not future formation during atrial mechanical dysfunction or early AF recurrence. A negative study does not remove the postconversion protection requirement in this five-day episode.'),
 ('Stop after twenty-four hours if the ECG remains sinus','Electrical rhythm restoration precedes reliable mechanical recovery and does not exclude silent recurrence. One day of sinus rhythm is not the recommended postconversion treatment interval.'),
 ('Continue indefinitely solely because cardioversion was performed','The early procedural requirement and the long-term stroke-risk indication are different. After the minimum period, a genuinely low-risk patient requires reassessment rather than indefinite treatment based only on having received a shock.')],
 [S('Electrical Recovery Is Not Immediate Mechanical Recovery','**Atrial stunning after cardioversion can sustain thromboembolic risk even when the ECG shows sinus rhythm.** Early AF recurrence can also occur without symptoms.',
    'US 2023 recommends therapeutic anticoagulation before cardioversion and continuation for at least ==4 weeks afterward== without interruption. This applies to the supplied episode lasting five days despite a negative TEE.',
    'Pharmacological conversion requires the same consideration of thromboembolic risk. Avoid treating a drug-induced restoration as a way around the precautions used for an electrical procedure.'),
  S('Separate the Minimum Course From Lifelong Prevention','**After the early course, reassess clinical stroke risk and any special indication.** A prior embolism, new hypertension, advancing age or another risk factor can warrant continuing treatment.',
    'This patient has no supplied chronic risk factor, so the question does not establish a lifelong indication. Conversely, a high-risk patient should not stop at four weeks merely because the procedural minimum has been completed.',
    'Very short, definitely timed episodes in selected low-risk patients have more nuanced guideline exceptions; they do not apply to a five-day episode and should not be generalized to uncertain onset. Confirm adherence, dose and safety during the early period and arrange follow-up for recurrence. Explain why a reassuring image and a normal rhythm can coexist with temporary continued anticoagulation, and why that temporary requirement is distinct from an automatically permanent prescription.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Structural Substrate and Class IC Drug Selection',
 'A patient with recurrent symptomatic AF has a prior myocardial infarction, ventricular scar and LVEF 35%. Which proposed rhythm-maintenance strategy should be avoided because of this substrate?',
 'Flecainide for long-term sinus-rhythm maintenance',[
 ('Amiodarone after review of organ toxicity and interactions','Amiodarone is a guideline-supported option in selected HFrEF patients, although substantial extracardiac toxicity and interactions require careful assessment. It is not restricted in the same way as flecainide by the supplied infarct-scar substrate.'),
 ('Dofetilide with protocol-based monitored initiation where available','Dofetilide can be used in selected HFrEF patients with renal, QT and interaction safeguards. Its initiation requirements are important, but it is not the class IC substrate-related exclusion tested here.'),
 ('Catheter-ablation evaluation after assessment of candidacy','Ablation can improve symptoms and selected outcomes in appropriate AF patients with HFrEF. Prior infarction does not itself rule out an electrophysiology assessment for this strategy.')],
 [S('Class IC Safety Depends on Myocardial Substrate','**Flecainide and propafenone are options for selected AF patients without prior MI or significant structural disease, scar or fibrosis.** They should not be used for rhythm maintenance in the infarct-scar/HFrEF presentation described.',
    'Potent sodium-channel blockade slows conduction and can produce ventricular proarrhythmia in diseased myocardium. Negative inotropy and conduction slowing add concern when systolic function is impaired.',
    'A normal pulse or a previously tolerated short dose does not establish chronic safety in a newly recognized high-risk substrate. Review ischemic history, ventricular function and conduction before prescribing.'),
  S('Choose a Compatible Rhythm Strategy','**Amiodarone or dofetilide can be appropriate pharmacological options in selected HFrEF patients under US guidance.** Their distinct toxicities and monitoring requirements must still be respected.',
    'Dofetilide requires monitored initiation, renal dosing and QT surveillance. Amiodarone requires review of thyroid, liver, pulmonary and other toxicity, as well as interactions with drugs such as warfarin and digoxin.',
    'Discuss ablation and continued HF treatment according to symptoms, rhythm burden and expected benefit. A decision against class IC treatment is not a decision that rate control is the only remaining option. When class IC drugs are appropriate in another patient, monitor QRS and conduction, renal status and interacting medicines, and use AV nodal protection to reduce rapid flutter conduction when indicated. Do not infer that a drug suitable for pharmacological conversion in one selected substrate is safe across all coronary or ventricular disease.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Pill-in-the-Pocket Conversion With Class IC Therapy',
 'A patient with infrequent symptomatic paroxysmal AF has no prior MI, significant structural disease, pre-excitation or conduction disorder. A single-dose flecainide strategy has been effective and safe during monitored testing. Which instruction is an essential component of the outpatient plan?',
 'Use the prescribed AV nodal blocker with the class IC dose to reduce the risk of one-to-one atrial flutter conduction',[
 ('Omit AV nodal treatment because flecainide reliably prevents rapid flutter conduction','Class IC treatment can organize AF into flutter while slowing its atrial cycle enough to permit rapid AV conduction. It does not reliably protect the AV node against a one-to-one response.'),
 ('Repeat the full conversion dose hourly until palpitations stop','The strategy is a prescribed single dose with a defined rescue plan, not unrestricted repeated loading. Repeated high doses can cause accumulation, QRS widening, bradycardia or ventricular proarrhythmia.'),
 ('Stop anticoagulation whenever the pill restores sinus rhythm','Conversion does not by itself remove the patient’s embolic-risk indication or pericardioversion precautions. Stroke prevention is assessed independently from symptom termination.')],
 [S('Select and Test the Patient Before Home Use','**Pill-in-the-pocket therapy uses a single oral flecainide or propafenone dose in carefully selected patients after monitored demonstration of safety and efficacy.** It suits infrequent recognized episodes rather than every AF presentation.',
    'Exclude prior MI, significant structural disease, important conduction disease and other contraindications. Evaluate sinus-node recovery and bradycardia risk after conversion, not only the probability of terminating AF.',
    'The patient needs reliable episode recognition and a clear plan for persistent symptoms, uncertain onset, chest pain, syncope or deterioration. Unstable episodes require emergency care rather than another home dose.'),
  S('Provide AV Nodal Protection and Exact Instructions','**A beta blocker or suitable nondihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker is generally given at least ==30 minutes before== the class IC dose under the US framework.** This reduces the risk of organized flutter conducting one-to-one.',
    'The US table lists single oral flecainide ==200 mg below 70 kg or 300 mg above 70 kg==, or propafenone ==450 mg below 70 kg or 600 mg above 70 kg==, subject to individual eligibility. These are conversion regimens, not routine maintenance doses or a reason to self-escalate.',
    'Review interactions and renal or hepatic impairment, and explain the maximum prescribed use and when to seek help. A prior successful monitored trial does not make new structural disease or medication changes irrelevant. Apply the appropriate anticoagulation and episode-duration rules because pharmacological conversion has thromboembolic implications. Reassess the strategy if attacks become frequent, less tolerated or difficult to identify; chronic treatment or ablation may then offer a better plan.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

R['DOFELABEL']=('Dofetilide US prescribing information','https://www.dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=cff8d66a-ed43-46dd-97de-3dd21a7f2572')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Dofetilide Initiation and Renal Dosing',
 'Dofetilide is selected for an eligible AF patient in a setting where it is available. Calculated creatinine clearance is 48 mL/min, baseline QTc is 420 ms with a narrow QRS, and potassium, magnesium and interaction review are satisfactory. Which initial approach matches US labelling?',
 '250 micrograms twice daily with protocol-based inpatient ECG monitoring',[
 ('500 micrograms twice daily with the same monitoring','Five hundred micrograms twice daily is the initial renal category above 60 mL/min when other criteria permit. At 48 mL/min, that dose exceeds the labelled starting renal category.'),
 ('125 micrograms twice daily as the standard renal starting category','This is the labelled starting category for clearance 20 to below 40 mL/min. A clinician may select a lower individualized dose, but it is not the standard category corresponding to the supplied clearance.'),
 ('250 micrograms twice daily with the first ECG check at an outpatient visit','The renal dose is correct, but initiation or reinitiation requires continuous monitored inpatient care with serial QT assessment and resuscitation capability. A correct milligram or microgram amount does not replace the required initiation setting.')],
 [S('Renal Clearance Determines the Starting Category','**Dofetilide dosing is based on calculated creatinine clearance and QT assessment.** The labelled category at ==40–60 mL/min== is ==250 micrograms twice daily==.',
    'The other starting categories are 500 micrograms twice daily above 60 mL/min and 125 micrograms twice daily at 20 to below 40 mL/min. Clearance below 20 mL/min is contraindicated.',
    'Baseline QTc above 440 ms generally excludes treatment, with a different 500-ms limit for ventricular conduction abnormalities. Use the label’s QT rather than QTc provision when heart rate is below 60/min and follow the full protocol.'),
  S('Prevent Concentration-Dependent Torsades','**Initiation requires at least three days of continuous ECG monitoring, and at least twelve hours after conversion if that is longer.** Serial postdose QT checks determine dose reduction or discontinuation.',
    'Check QTc/QT 2–3 hours after the first dose: an increase greater than 15% or a value above 500 ms, or 550 ms with conduction abnormality, requires the labelled dose reduction. Check 2–3 hours after subsequent inpatient doses. After the second dose, a value above the applicable 500/550-ms limit calls for discontinuation rather than further dose reduction.',
    'Correct potassium and magnesium, avoid interacting renal-cation-transport inhibitors and review other QT-prolonging drugs. Renal deterioration can raise exposure after a previously acceptable dose, so follow-up remains necessary. Dofetilide can be an option in selected HFrEF patients. The comparison below identifies different selection constraints; these drugs are not interchangeable simply because they affect repolarization.')],['DOFELABEL','AFUS2023'],
 table=(['Rhythm-maintenance drug','Selection and principal safeguards'],[
 ['Dofetilide','Selected HFrEF use; CrCl-based dose, inpatient initiation, serial QT; contraindicated at CrCl <20 mL/min'],
 ['Amiodarone','Selected HFrEF use; organ toxicity and interactions limit use; thyroid/liver and symptom-directed pulmonary surveillance'],
 ['Dronedarone','400 mg twice daily in eligible nonpermanent AF; avoid permanent AF, NYHA III–IV HF or decompensation within 4 weeks under US 2023 guidance'],
 ['Sotalol','Beta blockade plus class III effect; renal dose selection, QT/electrolyte and bradycardia surveillance; generally avoid in HFrEF under US 2023 guidance']]))

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Monitoring During Long-Term Amiodarone Treatment',
 'A patient taking amiodarone for AF reports progressive dry cough and dyspnea after several months. Examination and initial assessment do not establish congestion or infection. Which next approach best addresses an important treatment-related possibility?',
 'Evaluate for amiodarone pulmonary toxicity with chest imaging and clinical differential assessment',[
 ('Attribute symptoms to AF recurrence without checking pulmonary disease','AF can cause dyspnea, but a persistent dry cough and drug exposure require assessment of pulmonary causes. A rhythm explanation should be demonstrated rather than used to bypass a potentially serious adverse effect.'),
 ('Use normal thyroid tests to exclude clinically important amiodarone toxicity','Thyroid assessment detects one toxicity domain. Normal thyroid function does not exclude interstitial lung disease, hepatic injury or other amiodarone adverse effects.'),
 ('Continue unchanged treatment because a low maintenance dose excludes lung injury','Pulmonary toxicity can occur at lower maintenance doses and depends on cumulative exposure and susceptibility. Dose alone cannot safely exclude it in a symptomatic patient.')],
 [S('Investigate New Respiratory Symptoms','**Amiodarone can cause clinically important interstitial lung disease.** New unexplained cough or dyspnea warrants chest radiography and further assessment, with CT useful when indicated by the findings or persistent suspicion.',
    'Consider HF, infection, embolic disease and other pulmonary disorders in parallel. Drug exposure raises suspicion but is not itself proof of the diagnosis.',
    'If toxicity is suspected, obtain specialist input and reassess the need for continued amiodarone. Severe disease can require withdrawal and selected corticosteroid treatment after appropriate evaluation; a long tissue half-life means effects may persist after stopping.'),
  S('Monitor Multiple Organ Systems','**US AF guidance recommends baseline thyroid and liver testing, reassessment at ==3–6 months== and then about ==every 6 months==.** Baseline chest radiography and symptom-directed pulmonary investigation are also important.',
    'Review ECG for bradycardia, conduction changes and QT, and assess visual, neurological and skin symptoms. Routine CT in every asymptomatic patient is not the same recommendation as targeted CT for new unexplained respiratory symptoms.',
    'Check interactions with warfarin, digoxin and other relevant medicines. The drug’s rhythm efficacy and relatively lower torsades propensity do not make extracardiac toxicity negligible. Use the lowest effective appropriate regimen and periodically reconsider whether benefit still outweighs burden. Dronedarone lacks iodine but is not a universally safer replacement: permanent AF and certain HF settings are contraindicated, and liver injury remains possible. A normal result in one monitoring domain must not be used to dismiss symptoms in another.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

R['EAST']=('EAST-AFNET 4 randomized trial, 2020','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2019422')
add(SECTIONS[6][0],'Early Rhythm Control: EAST-AFNET 4',
 'A patient with AF diagnosed three months ago and cardiovascular risk factors asks what EAST-AFNET 4 established. Which interpretation is most accurate?',
 'An early rhythm-control strategy reduced a composite cardiovascular outcome compared with usual care in the studied population',[
 ('The comparison established the benefit of first-line ablation over first-line antiarrhythmic medication','The intervention was an early rhythm-control strategy using drugs and ablation as appropriate, not a randomized comparison between these two methods. The result cannot be assigned exclusively to ablation.'),
 ('Restoration of sinus rhythm removed the need for anticoagulation','Stroke prevention and cardiovascular care continued as indicated. The study does not establish rhythm success as permission to withdraw anticoagulation in a high-risk patient.'),
 ('The comparison primarily established a strict rate-control target in recently diagnosed AF','The randomized strategy concerned early restoration and maintenance of sinus rhythm, not strict versus lenient ventricular rate targets. Rate control could be used within care, but it was not the tested intervention.')],
 [S('Identify the Strategy and the Entry Window','**EAST-AFNET 4 tested early rhythm control in AF diagnosed within one year in patients with cardiovascular conditions.** Treatment could include antiarrhythmic medication or ablation, with care adapted over follow-up.',
    'Usual care emphasized symptom-directed rhythm treatment alongside appropriate cardiovascular management. This was not a comparison of ablation against no treatment or a test of rhythm control without anticoagulation.',
    'The trial informs discussion of rhythm control before years of progression, including patients whose symptoms alone may not fully capture the potential clinical benefit.'),
  S('Interpret the Outcome Without Enlarging the Claim','**The composite of cardiovascular death, stroke, or hospitalization for worsening HF or acute coronary syndrome was lower with early rhythm control.** The reported hazard ratio was 0.79, with a 96% confidence interval of 0.66–0.94.',
    'A composite benefit does not establish identical benefit for every individual component or prove that every patient needs the same drug or procedure. Treatment-related adverse events and patient suitability still matter.',
    'Continue stroke-risk treatment and comorbidity management independently of the selected rhythm approach. Consider structural disease, ventricular function, interactions, symptoms and preferences when choosing the method. A patient with longstanding permanent AF or major competing illness needs individualized assessment rather than a slogan that earlier is universally better. Explain both the population studied and the practical implication: early rhythm control is an evidence-based strategy to consider within comprehensive AF care, not a replacement for it.')],['EAST','AF2024','AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[6][0],'First-Line Catheter Ablation for Selected Paroxysmal AF',
 'A 49-year-old with recurrent symptomatic paroxysmal AF has few comorbidities, preserved ventricular function and a strong preference for rhythm control. No antiarrhythmic maintenance drug has yet been tried. Which counseling statement best matches US 2023 guidance?',
 'Catheter ablation is a valid first-line option after discussion of benefits, risks and alternatives',[
 ('Ablation requires failure of at least two antiarrhythmic drugs in every patient','Drug failure is not a universal prerequisite in selected symptomatic paroxysmal AF. Contemporary guidance supports first-line ablation in a suitable younger patient with few comorbidities.'),
 ('Ablation should be offered primarily as a guaranteed way to stop anticoagulation','Ablation can reduce symptoms and recurrence but does not guarantee permanent elimination of AF or remove a separate stroke-risk indication. Anticoagulation expectations should not be used as a guaranteed procedural promise.'),
 ('Ablation is reserved for patients who already have persistent AF or LV dysfunction','These can be important ablation settings, but selected symptomatic paroxysmal AF is itself an established indication. Waiting for progression is not a required selection step.')],
 [S('A First-Line Option Does Not Mean One Mandatory Treatment','**US 2023 supports first-line ablation in selected patients, generally younger with few comorbidities, with symptomatic paroxysmal AF who desire rhythm control.** It can improve symptoms and reduce progression to persistent AF.',
    'Compare an appropriate antiarrhythmic strategy, episodic treatment where suitable and ablation using shared decision-making. A well-informed preference is part of selection, not a substitute for anatomical and clinical assessment.',
    'First-line trial populations were relatively young and selected. Older patients or more complex substrates can still benefit, but expected outcomes and procedural burden require separate assessment.'),
  S('Set Realistic Expectations and Maintain Follow-Up','**Pulmonary vein isolation is the central procedural strategy for most AF ablation.** Recurrence, repeat procedures and temporary postprocedural arrhythmias can occur even after an initially successful intervention.',
    'Discuss vascular injury, tamponade, thromboembolism and energy-specific collateral risks, as well as local experience. Newer technology changes the risk profile but does not make the procedure risk free.',
    'Continue appropriate peri-procedural anticoagulation and subsequent risk assessment, and treat hypertension, obesity, alcohol exposure and other drivers. A normal postablation ECG is not proof of lifelong rhythm freedom. Assess success through symptoms and appropriate rhythm follow-up rather than one tracing alone. The decision in this vignette is that ablation may be offered without forcing drug failures first; it is not a claim of universal superiority for all patients or every outcome.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

R['CASTLE']=('CASTLE-AF randomized trial, 2018','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1707855')
R['ABLATION2024']=('EHRA/HRS/APHRS/LAHRS AF ablation consensus, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1002/joa3.13082')
add(SECTIONS[6][0],'AF Ablation in Selected Patients With HFrEF',
 'A patient with symptomatic AF, LVEF 30% and an implanted defibrillator remains symptomatic despite appropriate HF and rhythm-treatment assessment. Which statement accurately describes CASTLE-AF when discussing ablation?',
 'Ablation reduced death or HF hospitalization compared with medical therapy in the selected trial population',[
 ('The trial established an identical mortality benefit for every AF patient irrespective of EF','CASTLE-AF enrolled a selected HF population with LVEF no more than 35% and implanted defibrillator monitoring. It does not establish the same outcome effect across all AF or preserved-EF populations.'),
 ('The trial showed that ablation replaces guideline-directed HF medication','HF treatment remained part of care. Treating AF can improve the clinical course but does not remove the need to manage the underlying ventricular disease.'),
 ('The implanted defibrillator was unnecessary once ablation restored sinus rhythm','An ICD addresses a separately assessed ventricular-arrhythmia risk. AF rhythm improvement does not automatically eliminate that indication or establish a reason to remove device protection.')],
 [S('Know the Population Before Applying the Outcome','**CASTLE-AF enrolled symptomatic paroxysmal or persistent AF with HF, ==LVEF ≤35%== and an implanted defibrillator.** Patients had a relevant history of antiarrhythmic failure, intolerance or unwillingness to take such therapy.',
    'Ablation was compared with medical therapy in addition to HF care. Device monitoring supported assessment of rhythm burden, which differs from relying only on symptom reports.',
    'The selected population and specialist setting matter when discussing external validity. Atrial substrate, frailty, competing disease and procedural suitability can alter expected benefit.'),
  S('Connect Rhythm Treatment With HF Care','**The primary composite of death or HF hospitalization occurred in 28.5% after ablation versus 44.6% with medical therapy.** The hazard ratio was 0.62 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.43–0.87.',
    'Contemporary guidelines support ablation in appropriate HFrEF patients, particularly when AF contributes to symptoms or ventricular dysfunction. This is a clinical selection decision rather than a requirement that every low-EF patient undergo the procedure.',
    'Continue HF medication, stroke prevention and device follow-up as independently indicated. Evaluate whether an arrhythmia-induced component may recover after effective control, and reassess ventricular function and symptoms. Discuss recurrence and procedural risks alongside potential outcome benefit. Do not interpret rhythm improvement as proof that ischemic scar, underlying cardiomyopathy or ventricular sudden-death risk has disappeared. The trial supports integrating ablation into suitable HF care, not replacing the rest of that care.')],['CASTLE','AFUS2023','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[6][0],'Pulmonary Vein Isolation as the AF Ablation Foundation',
 'A patient with symptomatic paroxysmal AF undergoes a first catheter-ablation procedure. No separate specific trigger or macroreentrant tachycardia is identified. Which lesion strategy is the principal foundation?',
 'Electrical isolation of the pulmonary veins from the left atrium',[
 ('A CTI line alone','CTI ablation treats typical right atrial flutter. It does not isolate the pulmonary venous triggers that commonly initiate AF, though a separate documented flutter circuit can warrant treatment.'),
 ('AV-junction ablation alone','AV-junction ablation controls ventricular conduction by creating pacing dependence and does not eliminate atrial AF. It is a selected refractory rate-control strategy rather than AF rhythm ablation.'),
 ('Empirical left atrial linear lesions without pulmonary vein isolation','Additional lines can treat selected mapped circuits or substrates, but they do not replace the established PVI foundation in the supplied first-procedure setting. Incomplete lines can create organized reentry.')],
 [S('Isolate Triggering Myocardial Sleeves','**Pulmonary venous myocardial activity commonly initiates AF, particularly paroxysmal AF.** PVI aims to prevent electrical interaction between these sleeves and the left atrium.',
    'The therapeutic endpoint is electrical isolation rather than simply delivering a visually complete ring of lesions. The 2024 ablation consensus requires at least demonstration of entrance block into the veins.',
    'Exit block can be assessed by nonconducted pulmonary venous activity or suitable pacing. Distinguish local vein capture from far-field atrial capture when interpreting a pacing response.'),
  S('Add Lesions for a Defined Reason','**Additional ablation beyond PVI is not a universal requirement for every patient.** A mapped nonpulmonary trigger or another documented atrial tachycardia can justify targeted treatment.',
    'Evidence for routine empirical posterior-wall, fractionated-electrogram or other substrate strategies varies by population and technique. Avoid claiming that more atrial scar necessarily means more durable benefit.',
    'Incomplete lines can permit scar-related macroreentry, while excessive lesion burden adds procedural risk. Recurrent AF after a prior procedure requires assessment of reconnection and other mechanisms rather than assuming the same explanation in every patient. Energy selection changes how isolation is achieved but not the need to verify the intended effect. Continue appropriate anticoagulation and comorbidity care, and assess outcomes with symptoms and rhythm follow-up. Acute AF termination during ablation alone does not establish durable isolation or long-term success.')],['ABLATION2024','AFUS2023','AF2024'],style='direct')

R['ADVENT']=('ADVENT randomized trial, 2023','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2307291')
add(SECTIONS[6][0],'Pulsed-Field Versus Thermal AF Ablation',
 'A patient asks what the original randomized ADVENT trial established about pulsed-field ablation for drug-refractory paroxysmal AF. Which answer is most accurate?',
 'Pulsed-field ablation was noninferior to thermal ablation for the prespecified one-year efficacy and safety endpoints',[
 ('Pulsed-field ablation was superior for efficacy and noninferior for serious procedural safety events','ADVENT established noninferiority for the prespecified efficacy endpoint; a demonstrated superiority claim is a different statistical conclusion and was not the original trial result.'),
 ('Pulsed-field ablation was noninferior for efficacy and superior for serious procedural safety events','Relative tissue selectivity is clinically relevant, but the original randomized safety conclusion was noninferiority rather than superiority. Other access, embolic and perforation risks remain possible.'),
 ('Pulsed-field ablation was tested against medical rhythm control in persistent AF','ADVENT compared pulsed-field with thermal catheter ablation in drug-refractory paroxysmal AF. This option changes both the comparator and the enrolled AF pattern.')],
 [S('Understand the Energy Mechanism','**Pulsed-field ablation uses high-voltage electrical pulses to produce irreversible electroporation rather than relying principally on thermal injury.** Myocardial susceptibility can provide relative tissue selectivity.',
    'Radiofrequency creates thermal injury through heating, while cryothermal techniques use freezing. All require appropriate catheter positioning and confirmation of the intended electrical endpoint.',
    'Relative sparing of some adjacent tissues is an important advantage under investigation and clinical surveillance, but tissue selectivity is not an absolute guarantee across every waveform, catheter and application site.'),
  S('Interpret the Original ADVENT Comparison','**ADVENT compared PFA with radiofrequency or cryoballoon ablation in drug-refractory paroxysmal AF.** It met noninferiority criteria for a composite efficacy outcome and serious device/procedure-related safety events at one year.',
    'The efficacy composite included more than documented AF recurrence, so it should not be relabelled simply as permanent cure. Its original blanking definition also should not be assumed to be the current definition for every follow-up protocol.',
    'Device-specific experience and longer-term evidence matter when selecting a platform. PFA can have distinct hazards, including coronary spasm in relevant settings, and does not remove access, embolic or perforation risk. Avoid extrapolating one platform’s trial as proof that all PFA systems are interchangeable. Discuss center expertise, anatomy and patient priorities alongside the evidence. Continue anticoagulation and rhythm follow-up according to the clinical plan; a newer energy source does not independently settle those decisions.')],['ADVENT','ABLATION2024','AF2024'])

add(SECTIONS[6][0],'Atrioesophageal Fistula After AF Ablation',
 'Three weeks after left atrial radiofrequency ablation, a patient develops fever, painful swallowing and transient focal neurological deficits. Which immediate investigation strategy best addresses the most concerning procedural complication?',
 'Urgent contrast-enhanced chest CT with immediate specialist assessment for atrioesophageal fistula',[
 ('Upper GI endoscopy with routine air insufflation as the first test','Endoscopy may investigate many causes of dysphagia, but air insufflation in suspected atrioesophageal communication can cause catastrophic embolism. It is not the appropriate first investigation here.'),
 ('TEE as the first test to exclude an embolic source','TEE can investigate atrial thrombus and valve disease, but esophageal instrumentation is hazardous when a fistula is suspected. The recent ablation, fever and swallowing symptoms direct assessment toward chest CT and urgent specialist care.'),
 ('Treat presumed uncomplicated postablation pericarditis and reassess if symptoms persist','Pericarditis can cause pain after ablation, but the delayed combination of fever, dysphagia and neurological events raises a life-threatening fistula concern. Symptomatic treatment alone would delay essential diagnosis.')],
 [S('Recognize the Delayed Red Flags','**Atrioesophageal fistula is a rare, potentially fatal delayed complication of left atrial thermal ablation.** Fever, chest or swallowing pain, GI bleeding and neurological events can occur days to weeks afterward.',
    'Communication permits systemic air or septic embolization and can produce fluctuating neurological signs or sepsis. The full classical presentation need not be present before suspicion is raised.',
    'Ask specifically about recent ablation when assessing unexplained neurological symptoms and fever. A routine discharge course without immediate complications does not exclude a delayed injury.'),
  S('Image Urgently and Avoid Hazardous Instrumentation','**Urgent contrast chest CT is the principal initial imaging approach when fistula is suspected.** Obtain immediate electrophysiology, cardiothoracic and other relevant specialist involvement.',
    'A negative initial CT does not conclusively exclude an evolving fistula when suspicion remains high; repeat assessment and imaging may be needed. Avoid routine esophageal instrumentation and endoscopy with air insufflation.',
    'Specialized CO2 endoscopy has been described in carefully selected expert pathways after nondiagnostic imaging, but it is not an initial generalist substitute for CT or surgical consultation. Confirmed disease generally requires urgent definitive management, with surgery a central consideration. Distinguish other postablation emergencies: acute hypotension can suggest tamponade, while later dyspnea or hemoptysis can raise pulmonary vein stenosis. The delayed febrile swallowing-neurological pattern here is particularly concerning for atrioesophageal communication.')],['ABLATION2024','AFUS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[6][0],'Early Atrial Arrhythmia Recurrence After AF Ablation',
 'Two weeks after AF ablation, a stable patient has several documented symptomatic AF episodes. There is no fever, swallowing pain, neurological deficit, hypotension or HF. Which interpretation best guides care?',
 'Early recurrence warrants assessment and symptom treatment but does not by itself establish durable ablation failure',[
 ('Any recurrence in the first month proves permanent procedural failure','Early arrhythmias can arise during lesion maturation, inflammation and transient autonomic change and may subsequently settle. A single early episode does not establish the final rhythm outcome.'),
 ('The blanking period means documented episodes should receive no assessment or treatment','Blanking is an outcome-classification convention, not a direction to ignore symptoms or complications. Rate control, selected antiarrhythmic therapy or cardioversion may be appropriate after evaluation.'),
 ('Immediate repeat ablation is required for every early episode','Repeat intervention can be considered in selected refractory or highly symptomatic cases. It is not a universal response to stable early recurrence, and timing should reflect mechanism, burden and clinical course.')],
 [S('Understand What Blanking Means','**An early postablation episode is not automatically counted as definitive long-term failure.** Transient biological changes can promote arrhythmia before lesions and atrial function stabilize.',
    'The 2024 multisociety ablation consensus recommends an ==8-week blanking period==. Older studies, including the original ADVENT protocol, used three months, so study definitions and contemporary recommendations must be distinguished.',
    'Blanking does not mean recurrence has no prognostic information. Frequent or later early episodes can predict subsequent recurrence more strongly than an isolated very early event.'),
  S('Treat the Patient During the Observation Period','**Document the rhythm, burden, symptoms and ventricular response.** Exclude an urgent complication when fever, neurological signs, swallowing symptoms, hypotension or progressive dyspnea appears.',
    'Use appropriate rate treatment, selected short-term antiarrhythmic therapy or cardioversion when clinically indicated. Temporary drug suppression can reduce early symptoms without proving that late recurrence has been prevented.',
    'Continue the prescribed postablation antithrombotic plan: ESC 2024 specifies at least two months after AF ablation, while US 2023 specifies at least three months; subsequent treatment requires a separate risk-based assessment. Neither interval is the eight-week rhythm-outcome blanking definition. Reassess after the early period using appropriate rhythm monitoring. Persistent symptomatic recurrence may warrant repeat mapping, including assessment of pulmonary-vein reconnection or scar-related circuits. A symptom-free week does not establish that anticoagulation can stop, and an early episode does not guarantee permanent failure.')],['ABLATION2024','AFUS2023','AF2024'])

R['ALONEAF']=('ALONE-AF randomized trial, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2025.14679')
R['OCEAN']=('OCEAN randomized trial, online 2025; print 2026','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2509688')
add(SECTIONS[6][0],'Anticoagulation Evidence After Apparently Successful AF Ablation',
 'A patient asks whether ALONE-AF and OCEAN justify stopping anticoagulation three weeks after AF ablation because recent ECGs show sinus rhythm. Which interpretation best distinguishes the trial evidence from this situation?',
 'The trials concern selected patients without documented recurrence for at least a year; they do not establish an early postprocedural stopping strategy',[
 ('The trials establish that a negative ECG at three weeks is sufficient evidence to withdraw anticoagulation','An office ECG can document the current rhythm, but it does not reproduce the prolonged recurrence-free selection in these trials. Early healing and atrial dysfunction create a separate procedural-risk period.'),
 ('OCEAN establishes aspirin as equivalent to anticoagulation for AF stroke prevention','OCEAN did not demonstrate rivaroxaban superiority for its primary endpoint in a selected late-postablation population. A nonsignificant superiority test does not establish equivalence or justify aspirin substitution in ordinary clinical AF.'),
 ('ALONE-AF establishes that discontinuation reduces ischemic stroke compared with continued anticoagulation','Its primary outcome combined stroke, systemic embolism and major bleeding. A lower combined event rate with discontinuation does not establish an independently significant reduction in ischemic stroke or universal thromboembolic safety.')],
 [S('Separate Early Procedural Protection From Late Reassessment','**The early postablation anticoagulation course addresses a different question from indefinite treatment after prolonged apparent rhythm success.** ESC 2024 recommends at least two months after AF ablation and US 2023 at least three months, with subsequent therapy based on the applicable stroke-risk framework.',
    'Sinus rhythm on several ECGs does not exclude silent intermittent recurrence. Neither a symptom-free interval nor the eight-week blanking convention independently establishes a safe anticoagulation stop date.',
    'The 2024 ablation consensus already distinguished low-, intermediate- and higher-risk patients, allowing consideration of later discontinuation in selected intermediate-risk patients after shared decision-making and a commitment to rhythm surveillance.'),
  S('Interpret the Newer Trials Within Their Selection Criteria','**ALONE-AF randomized selected patients with stroke-risk factors and no documented atrial-arrhythmia recurrence for at least one year after ablation to discontinue or continue oral anticoagulation.** The two-year composite of stroke, systemic embolism and major bleeding favored discontinuation, with bleeding important to the net outcome.',
    'OCEAN compared rivaroxaban with aspirin in selected patients at least one year after successful ablation. Rivaroxaban did not significantly reduce its composite of clinical stroke, systemic embolism or a qualifying new covert embolic cerebral infarct. Different comparators and endpoints prevent treating the two trials as identical experiments.',
    'These results inform individualized late reassessment, but sparse ischemic events limit certainty in very-high-risk groups. Absence of a statistically significant difference is not proof that two strategies are equivalent. Consider previous embolism, comorbidity, bleeding, recurrence ascertainment and patient preferences, and distinguish emerging trial evidence from the earlier guideline wording. A decision to discontinue requires follow-up and a plan to reassess prevention if AF recurs or risk changes. This patient is only three weeks after ablation, so the late recurrence-free trials do not answer the proposed immediate withdrawal question.')],['ALONEAF','OCEAN','AF2024','AFUS2023','ABLATION2024'])

add(SECTIONS[6][0],'AV-Junction Ablation With Pacing for Refractory AF Rate Control',
 'A patient has persistent symptomatic rapid AF despite maximally tolerated rate drugs. Rhythm-control procedures have failed and further atrial ablation is not considered appropriate. LVEF is 30%. Which procedural strategy is reasonable to discuss?',
 'AV-junction ablation with an appropriate resynchronization or physiologic pacing plan',[
 ('AV-junction ablation without arranging dependable pacing','The procedure intentionally interrupts atrioventricular conduction and creates pacing dependence. Reliable pacing must be established and its function verified rather than relying on an unpredictable escape rhythm.'),
 ('Conventional RV pacing as an automatically equivalent choice regardless of ventricular function','RV pacing can provide rate support, but near-continuous pacing with reduced EF raises dyssynchrony concerns. CRT or selected conduction-system approaches require assessment rather than assuming all ventricular activation strategies are equivalent.'),
 ('CTI ablation as the substitute for AV-junction treatment','CTI ablation treats a demonstrated typical flutter circuit. It does not reliably control AV conduction during persistent AF and is not the indicated substitute when the unresolved problem is refractory ventricular rate.')],
 [S('Separate Rate Ablation From AF Rhythm Ablation','**AV-junction ablation controls ventricular rate by preventing atrial impulses from reaching the ventricle.** AF can continue in the atria, so the procedure does not restore atrial contraction or eliminate embolic risk.',
    'It can improve symptoms and quality of life when rate treatment is inadequate and rhythm control has failed or is unsuitable. This is a selected strategy with irreversible pacing dependence, not routine first-line treatment for uncomplicated AF.',
    'Discuss device complications, follow-up, generator changes and the underlying ventricular disease before proceeding. Establish reliable pacing before or in conjunction with the ablation according to the clinical plan.'),
  S('Choose Ventricular Activation and Initial Rate Carefully','**Reduced EF and anticipated continuous pacing favor assessment of CRT or an appropriate physiologic strategy.** Conduction-system pacing can be an alternative in selected settings, but evidence and technical considerations differ from established biventricular CRT.',
    'US 2023 recommends an initial lower pacing rate of ==80–90/min== after AV nodal ablation to reduce sudden-death risk. Subsequent rate reduction is individualized; do not immediately apply a low routine backup rate.',
    'For an existing CRT patient, AF can reduce effective biventricular capture; AV-junction ablation may help when appropriate medication cannot secure adequate pacing. Verify effective capture rather than relying on a device counter alone. Continue HF therapy and stroke prevention as independently indicated, and monitor ventricular function and clinical response. A technically successful AV block is only one part of success: dependable appropriate ventricular activation and improved clinical status are the therapeutic objectives.')],['AFUS2023','AF2024'])

if __name__=='__main__':
    count=sum(len(v) for _,v in SECTIONS);decisions=[(k,d)for k,items in SECTIONS for d in items]
    assert len(rows)==count
    for row,(topic,decision)in zip(rows,decisions):assert row['topic']==topic;row['coverageDecision']=decision
    p=save('Cardiology',6,DOC,rows,R,'Original postgraduate educational synthesis with source checks and author editorial review. Full Harrison text not accessed; independent clinician review not claimed.')
    b=json.loads(p.read_text(encoding='utf-8'));b.update(status='chapter_authored',plannedQuestions=count);p.write_text(json.dumps(b,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
    c=dict(subject='Cardiology',chapterNumber=6,chapter=DOC,status='coverage_reviewed',countPolicy='Coverage-driven distinct decisions; not official examination weights.',plannedQuestions=count,authoredQuestions=len(rows),sections=[dict(section=k,questions=len(v),decisions=v)for k,v in SECTIONS])
    c['questionDecisions']=[dict(question=i,title=r['title'],decision=r['coverageDecision'],section=r['topic'])for i,r in enumerate(rows,1)]
    (p.parent/(DOC+'.coverage.json')).write_text(json.dumps(c,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
    pp=ROOT/'progress.json';plan=json.loads(pp.read_text(encoding='utf-8'));entry=next(x for x in plan['chapters']if x['subject']=='Cardiology'and x['document']==DOC);entry.update(status='chapter_authored',authoredQuestions=len(rows),plannedQuestions=count);pp.write_text(json.dumps(plan,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
